WN.lb(4,{"mt_1KCwbGvm1F":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which way does your finger move along a line of words?","choices":["left to right →","right to left ←","bottom to top ↑"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at the first word of the line. Which way is the next word?","explain":"In English we read each line from left to right, from the first word to the last."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which one is a single letter?","choices":["m","map","mum"],"answer":0,"hint":"A letter is one shape on its own. A word has letters side by side.","explain":"m is one letter. Map and mum are words, each made of three letters."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia writes {n} words in a line, with a gap between each pair. How many gaps does she leave?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Try it with two words first: how many gaps are between them?","explain":"Each gap sits between two words, so {n} words need {=n-1} gaps.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What tells you the name of a book?","choices":["the title on the front cover","the barcode on the back","the last word on the last page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the front of the book, near the top.","explain":"The title is on the front cover. It tells you what the book is called."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How many words are in: I like dogs?","choices":["3","9","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tap once under each group of letters. Do not count the letters.","explain":"I, like, dogs: 3 words. There are 9 letters, but letters are not words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A page has {n} lines of words. Which line do you read first?","choices":["the top line","the bottom line","the longest line"],"answer":0,"hint":"We read down the page, one line after another. Where does down start?","explain":"We read the top line first, then each line below it in turn.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Do the words in a book say the same thing every time you read them?","choices":["yes, the words stay the same","no, they change each time","only if you look at the picture"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a story you know well. Does the grown-up read the same words each time?","explain":"Print does not change, so the words always say the same thing. The picture helps, but the words carry the story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You read the last word on a line. What do you do next?","choices":["go back to the left and read the next line","stop, because the page is done","read the same line again backwards"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there another line below? Where does it start?","explain":"We sweep back to the left end of the next line and keep reading until the last line."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where do you start reading a page?","choices":["the top line, on the left","the bottom line, on the right","in the middle of the page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the very first word. Which line is it on, and which side?","explain":"English starts at the top line, at the left end, and moves along to the right."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A line has {n} words. How many gaps are there between the words?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Two words have one gap between them. Count the gaps between each pair of words.","explain":"There is a gap between each pair of words, so {n} words have {=n-1} gaps. There is always one more word than gaps.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Is dog a letter or a word?","choices":["a word","a letter","a number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the letters in it. One letter on its own is a letter.","explain":"Dog has three letters side by side, d, o and g, so it is a word."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam reads the {n} words on the top line. He gets to the end. Where does he read next?","choices":["at the left end of the next line","straight down, at the right end of the next line","back along the top line"],"answer":0,"hint":"After each line your eyes sweep back to one side of the page. Which side?","explain":"At the end of a line we sweep back to the left and read the next line from its first word.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"The top line has {a} words. The next line has {b} words. How many words are on the page?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Point under each word on both lines and count as you go.","explain":"{a} words and {b} more words make {=a+b} words on the page.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["The words on a page tell us something. They say the same thing each time we read them.","We start at the top, on the left. Then we read along the line this way →.","At the end of a line, we jump back to the left. Then we read the next line down.","There is a gap after each word. A word is made of letters. Cat is one word. It has three letters."],"adult":["Concepts of print are the ideas a child needs before they can learn to read: print carries a fixed message, English runs left to right and top to bottom, each line ends with a sweep back to the left of the next line, and spaces mark where words begin and end.","Point under each word as you read aloud, one tap for each spoken word. This one-to-one matching shows that a spoken word matches a written word, and it helps the child see the gaps. Let the child take over the pointing on a familiar book.","Common mistakes: reading the picture instead of the words, starting on the right or in the middle, going straight down at the end of a line, and counting letters as words. Children also mix up the words letter and word, so name both: this is the letter s, this is the word sun.","Show the front cover, the title and which way the pages turn. Ask where to start and let the child point. Arabic and Hebrew run right to left, so the direction is a rule of English writing, not of all writing."]},"familyTip":"At story time, ask your child to show you the front of the book and where to start. Then guide their finger under each word as you read it aloud.","keyIdea":"Print tells us something, and it says the same thing every time. In English we read from the top line down, and along each line from left to right. At the end of a line we jump back to the left. Gaps split the words, and each word is made of letters.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Letter or word? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Letter","Word"],"items":[["s",0],["sun",1],["t",0],["top",1],["p",0],["pen",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagprint","caption":"Flagship lab: slide a reading finger along the lines, count words, sort letters from words and turn a book to its front.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_UvNrOXny1i":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many beats in elephant?","choices":["3 beats","2 beats","8 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly: el, e, phant. Clap each part.","explain":"El-e-phant has 3 beats. Eight is how many letters it has, which is not the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Strong has 6 letters. How many beats does it have?","choices":["1 beat","6 beats","2 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say strong. Do you need to stop in the middle?","explain":"Strong is said in one go, so it has 1 beat, even though it has 6 letters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Cake has the letters a and e. How many beats?","choices":["1 beat","2 beats","4 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say cake. Is the e at the end loud or quiet?","explain":"The e at the end of cake is silent, so there is only one vowel sound and 1 beat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What word do pop and corn make?","choices":["popcorn","cornpop","pop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say first pop, then corn, quickly together.","explain":"Pop + corn in that order makes popcorn. Gluing beats in the order you hear them builds the word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where does thunder break into beats?","choices":["thun | der","th | under","thund | er"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly and listen for where your voice stops for a moment.","explain":"You hear thun, then der, so thunder splits as thun | der, with one vowel sound in each piece."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mia says ba-na-na and claps {n} times. She says banana has {n} beats. Is she right?","choices":["No, banana has 3 beats","Yes, she is right","No, banana has 6 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say ba-na-na and count the parts on your fingers.","explain":"Ba-na-na has three parts, so it has 3 beats. Six is the number of letters.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ben has {n} picture cards. He wants one with 1 beat, like dog. Which card?","choices":["train","tiger","apple"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clap each word. Which one needs only one clap?","explain":"Train is said in one go, so it has 1 beat. Ti-ger and ap-ple have two beats each.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How many beats in caterpillar?","choices":["4 beats","3 beats","11 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly: cat, er, pil, lar.","explain":"Cat-er-pil-lar has 4 beats. Eleven is the number of letters, not beats."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Say butterfly slowly and clap. How many beats?","choices":["3 beats","2 beats","9 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it in parts: but, then ter, then fly. Clap for each part.","explain":"But-ter-fly has three parts, so it has 3 beats (syllables). Nine is the number of letters, not beats."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Kit claps {n} times for cat. Is that right?","choices":["No, cat has 1 beat","Yes, that is right","No, cat has 3 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say cat. Do you say it in one go, or in parts?","explain":"Cat is said in one go, so it has 1 beat. Its three letters do not make three beats.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What word do rain and bow make?","choices":["rainbow","bowrain","rain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the parts in order: first rain, then bow, quickly.","explain":"Gluing (blending) rain + bow in order makes rainbow. The order matters: bowrain is not a word."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam claps two-beat words, like rab-bit. He claps {n} words. Which word could be next?","choices":["pen-cil","dog","but-ter-fly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clap each word. Which one has two claps, like rab-bit?","explain":"Pen-cil has two beats, like rab-bit. Dog has one beat and but-ter-fly has three.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Where does carrot break into beats?","choices":["car | rot","ca | rrot","carr | ot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say carrot slowly. What do you hear first?","explain":"You say car, then rot, so the beats are car | rot. Each piece has one vowel sound."}],"explain":{"kid":["Words have beats. Say cat. Clap one time. Cat has one beat.","Say rab-bit. Clap two times. Rab-bit has two beats. A beat is called a syllable.","Some long words have one beat. Strong has lots of letters. Say it. Clap one time!","You can glue beats. Rain and bow make rainbow. You can cut a word too: rab and bit."],"adult":["A syllable is a beat of sound in a spoken word, built around one vowel sound. Counting, blending and splitting syllables is an early step in hearing the sound structure of words, before and alongside phonics. It helps children read longer words in chunks and spell them part by part (the English national curriculum asks Year 1 children to divide words such as rabbit, carrot, thunder and sunset into syllables).","In the lab, your child claps words such as cat, rabbit and butterfly, then compares strong with banana (six letters each, one beat against three). They glue spoken parts into rainbow, popcorn and dinosaur, chop written words at the beat breaks, and sort pictures into one, two or three beats.","Common mistakes: counting letters instead of beats, counting every vowel letter (cake has a and e but one beat, because the final e is silent), and rushing so two beats blur into one. A useful trick is to put a hand under the chin and say the word slowly: the chin drops once for each beat.","Clap names at home. Your child's name, a sibling's, the dog's: how many beats? Then say a word in beats, such as ta-ble, and let them glue it back together."]},"familyTip":"Play a clapping name game: clap each family member's name, one clap per beat (Sam, Ma-ya, Ol-i-ver). Then say a word in parts, like rain ... bow, and ask your child to say the whole word.","keyIdea":"A syllable is one beat in a spoken word. Cat has one beat, rab-bit has two and but-ter-fly has three. You can clap the beats, glue beats together to make a word, and split a written word into its beats to help you read and spell it. Count beats by listening, not by counting letters: strong has six letters but only one beat.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"One beat or two? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["1 beat","2 beats"],"items":[["cat",0],["dog",0],["train",0],["cake",0],["rabbit",1],["pencil",1],["apple",1],["tiger",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsyl","caption":"Flagship lab: clap the beats, find that letters are not beats, glue parts into words, chop written words at the beats, then sort pictures by beats.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_frDIaXzWbx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the name of the letter h?","choices":["aitch","huh","ay"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the ABC song, not the sound at the start of hat.","explain":"The name of h is aitch. huh is the sound it makes in hat, and ay is the name of a."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Turn the letter d around, like in a mirror. Which letter do you get?","choices":["b","d","p"],"answer":0,"hint":"d has its tummy first. Where is the tummy after the flip?","explain":"After the flip the stick comes first and the tummy after it, so d turns into b."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. To spell it out loud you say one letter name for each letter. How many names do you say?","answer":"n","hint":"One letter, one name. Count the letters.","explain":"{n} letters means you say {n} letter names, one for each letter.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam sings l-m-n-o-p as one word. How many letters is that really?","choices":["5","1","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each one slowly and hold up a finger for each name.","explain":"l, m, n, o and p are five letters. The song goes fast, so they can sound like one word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Is it c or k at the start of the word cat?","choices":["c, see","k, kay","Either one is fine"],"answer":0,"hint":"c and k can both say k. Think of how cat is spelt: see, ay, ...","explain":"cat is spelt see, ay, tee. k can make the same sound, but cat always uses c."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Ali can say the alphabet up to letter number {n}. Z is letter number 26. How many letters are left?","answer":"26-n","hint":"Count on from {n} to 26, one letter at a time.","explain":"From letter {n} to letter 26 there are 26 take away {n}, which is {=26-n} letters left.","params":{"n":[12,25]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which pair is a capital and small letter of the same letter?","choices":["A and a","B and d","P and q"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the name of each shape. Do they match?","explain":"A and a are both called ay. B and d, and P and q, are different letters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which letter comes just before S in the alphabet?","choices":["R","T","Q"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sing the ABC from P: p, q, and stop before s.","explain":"The order is q, r, s, t, so R comes just before S. T comes after it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which small letter is the partner of capital G?","choices":["g","q","c"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the name of capital G. Which small letter has the same name, gee?","explain":"G and g are one letter called gee. q has a tail too, but its name is cue."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The alphabet has 26 letters. Mia can name {n} of them. How many more letters does she need to learn?","answer":"26-n","hint":"Start at {n} and count on to 26, or take {n} away from 26.","explain":"26 take away {n} is {=26-n}, so Mia has {=26-n} more letters to learn.","params":{"n":[10,24]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which letter comes just after K in the alphabet?","choices":["L","J","M"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sing the ABC from H: h, i, j, k, ...","explain":"The order is j, k, l, m, so L comes just after K. J comes before it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Each letter has a capital and a small shape. How many shapes do {n} letters have?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each letter gives two shapes. Count two for every letter.","explain":"Two shapes for each of {n} letters makes {=2*n} shapes, but still only {n} letter names.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Every letter has a name. This is b. Its name is bee. We sing the names in the ABC song. Each letter has two shapes. A big one is a capital. A small one is for most words. G and g are the same letter.","Look at b and d. Turn b around and it is d! So check which way it faces. b has the stick first. d has its tummy first.","The letters go in order, A to Z. Sing it slowly: l, m, n, o, p. That is five letters, not one long word.","A letter has a name and a sound. c is called see. In cat it says k. To spell cat, say the names: see, ay, tee."],"adult":["This is alphabet knowledge, one of the strongest early predictors of learning to read. US Common Core RF.K.1.d asks children to recognise and name all upper- and lowercase letters. The English national curriculum for Year 1 asks pupils to name the letters of the alphabet in order and to use letter names to tell apart alternative spellings of the same sound.","Keep three ideas apart. The letter name is fixed (aitch for h). The letter sound, or phoneme, is what the letter usually says in a word (h as in hat). Phonics lessons teach the sounds first for reading, so children often know sounds before names. Both are needed: names stay the same while one sound can have several spellings, so names let you talk about spelling, for example k or c.","Common mistakes: reversing b, d, p and q, because in everyday life an object turned around is still the same object (mirror generalisation); hearing l-m-n-o-p in the song as one letter, elemeno; thinking a capital and its lowercase letter are two different letters, especially pairs that look different such as A and a, G and g, R and r; and giving the sound when asked for the name.","In the lab your child drags small letters to their capital partners, finds b, d, p and q, fills gaps in an alphabet train, picks a letter's name over its sound, and spells cat, kid and dog with letter names. Be careful to say names and sounds clearly and not to mix them in one sentence."]},"familyTip":"Play I spy with letter names on signs and cereal boxes: 'I spy a capital M, em.' Then ask your child to find its small partner, m, somewhere else on the box.","keyIdea":"The alphabet has 26 letters. Each letter has a name, like bee for b, and two shapes: a capital and a small letter. Capital G and small g are one letter with one name. The letters come in a set order, from A to Z. Some letters are the same shape turned around, like b and d, so which way a letter faces matters. We use letter names to spell, for example cat is see, ay, tee.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which capital is each small letter's partner? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A","G","R"],"items":[["a",0],["ɑ",0],["g",1],["ɡ",1],["r",2],["R",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagabc","caption":"Flagship lab: match small letters to capitals, find b, d, p and q, fill the alphabet train, tell names from sounds, and spell with letter names.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YKkCM63fSC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Can you sound out the word and?","choices":["Yes: a-n-d","No, it is a tricky word","No, you have to guess it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the sound of each letter: a, n, d.","explain":"Every letter in and says its usual sound, so it sounds out. It is common, but not tricky."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do we write the word that sounds like sed?","choices":["said","sed","sad"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is a tricky word. The middle is not written the way it sounds.","explain":"We write said. The ai in the middle says e: that is its tricky part."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"You know {n} tricky words. This week you learn {k} more. How many tricky words do you know now?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Start at {n} and count on {k}.","explain":"{n} and {k} more makes {=n+k} tricky words you can read in a blink.","params":{"n":[5,12],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which part of the word you is tricky?","choices":["The ou, it says oo","The y at the start","None, it sounds out"],"answer":0,"hint":"y says y as usual. What do the next two letters say?","explain":"The ou in you says oo, not its usual sound. That is the bit to learn by heart."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You are stuck on the word they. What helps most?","choices":["Sound out th, then learn that ey says ay","Guess from the picture","Skip the word"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use what you know first, then the tricky part.","explain":"th says th as usual. The ey says ay: learn that part by heart and read they lots of times."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A sentence has {n} words. You read {k} of them by sight. How many words do you still need to sound out?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the sight words away from all the words.","explain":"{n} words take away {k} sight words leaves {=n-k} words to sound out.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Was and saw use the same three letters. Are they the same word?","choices":["No, the order is different","Yes, same letters","Yes, if you read fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first letter of each word.","explain":"The order of the letters makes the word. Was starts with w, saw starts with s."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In the word of, which letter does not say its usual sound?","choices":["The f, it says v","The o","Both letters"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say of slowly. Does the end sound like f in fish?","explain":"The f in of says v, like in van. That is the tricky part of of."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Sight words: which part of the word said is the tricky part?","choices":["The ai, it says e","The s at the start","The d at the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each part. Which one does not make its usual sound?","explain":"s says s and d says d, as usual. The ai says e here, so the ai is the tricky part to learn by heart."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A page has {n} words. {k} of them are the word the. How many words on the page are not the?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the number of the words away from all the words.","explain":"{n} words take away {k} is {=n-k} words that are not the. The word the is so common it is worth knowing in a blink.","params":{"n":[20,30],"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The letters are w, a, s. Which word is it?","choices":["was","saw","wish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at the first letter. Which word starts with w?","explain":"w, a, s is was. Saw has the same letters in a new order, so it starts with s."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You read the word said {n} times on each page of a book. How many times do you read it on 2 pages?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count the times on one page, then the same again for the next page.","explain":"{n} on one page and {n} on the next is {=2*n} times. Reading a word that often helps you know it at a glance.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some words are in every book. The, said, was, you. If you know them fast, reading feels easy.","Look at said. The s says s. The d says d. But the ai says e, not ai. That bit is the tricky part. Put a heart on it and learn it by heart.","Was and saw have the same letters. But the letters go in a new order, so it is a new word. Look at every letter, from the start.","Not every word we see a lot is tricky. And, it and can sound out. Read them, and the tricky ones, again and again. Soon you know them in a blink."],"adult":["This is word reading in early phonics. The National Curriculum for Year 1 asks children to read common exception words, noting unusual correspondences between spelling and sound and where these occur in the word. Common Core RF.K.3c asks for common high-frequency words read by sight, and RF.1.3g for irregularly spelled words. Phonics programmes often call these tricky words. By 5 to 8, the aim is to read the common ones instantly in connected text.","Reading by sight is not memorising a word's shape. Ehri's work on orthographic mapping shows that a word becomes a sight word when its letters are linked to its sounds and stored. So teach the decodable part through each grapheme-phoneme correspondence (GPC). Then mark the irregular grapheme, the heart part, as the bit to learn by heart. Many high-frequency words, such as and, it and can, are fully decodable. Automaticity comes from many quick, accurate reads, then reading the word in sentences.","Common mistakes: treating every high-frequency word as irregular, so children stop using phonics; guessing from the first letter or the picture, which mixes up was and saw; drilling long lists of flashcards that never appear in real reading; and spelling a tricky word the way it sounds, like sed or thay. Be careful with too many new words at once. Three to five a week, mixed with known ones, works better.","In the lab your child taps the tricky part in said, was, you and the, then catches flashed words and picks was over saw. They build said, was and they from letter tiles that include the sound-alike traps. Then they find the words in real sentences and sort common words into sound it out and tricky part."]},"familyTip":"Write three tricky words on sticky notes and draw a small heart over the tricky part, like the ai in said. Hide them round the room. When your child finds one, they read it, then you both spot it in a bedtime book.","keyIdea":"Some words come up in almost every book: the, said, was, you, they. Good readers know them at a glance, without sounding them out each time. Many of these words still sound out, like and or it. Some have a tricky part that does not say its usual sound, like the ai in said, which says e. Sound out the rest of the word, learn the tricky part by heart, and read the word lots of times until it is quick.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Can you sound it out, or does it have a tricky part? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Sound it out","Has a tricky part"],"items":[["it",0],["said",1],["can",0],["you",1],["and",0],["was",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsight","caption":"Flagship lab: tap the tricky part of a word, catch flashed words, build tricky words from tiles, spot them in sentences and sort them.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_PvU3eoikev":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the rime in frog?","choices":["og","rog","g"],"answer":0,"hint":"The rime starts at the vowel. Find the vowel first.","explain":"f and r blend together as the onset, fr. The rime is the vowel and what follows: og."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Blend s + un. What word is it?","choices":["sun","nus","sin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the onset first, then the rime, quickly: s, un.","explain":"s first, then un, said fast together, makes sun."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"The -at family has cat, hat and mat. You add {n} more -at words, like sat and bat. How many -at words is that now?","answer":"3+n","hint":"Start at 3 for cat, hat and mat, then count on.","explain":"3 words and {n} more makes {=3+n} words, all with the same rime, at.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the middle sound in pen?","choices":["e","p","n"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly: p, e, n. Which sound is between the other two?","explain":"p is first, e is in the middle and n is last. The middle sound is e."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word is in the same word family as hug?","choices":["mug","hat","bag"],"answer":0,"hint":"A word family shares the same rime. What is the rime in hug?","explain":"hug has the rime ug, and so does mug. hat starts like hug, but its rime is at; bag has the rime ag. So only mug is in the -ug family."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A CVC word has 3 sounds. You change 1 sound in each of {n} words to make new words. How many sounds stay the same?","answer":"2*n","hint":"In each word, 1 sound changes and the other 2 stay.","explain":"2 sounds stay in each word, and {n} words, makes {=2*n} sounds that stay the same.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Change the last sound in cat to p. What word is it?","choices":["cap","pat","cot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Only the last sound changes. Keep c and a.","explain":"Keep c and a, and change t to p: cap."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How do you split chin into onset and rime?","choices":["ch + in","c + hin","chi + n"],"answer":0,"hint":"c and h make one sound here. The rime starts at the vowel i.","explain":"ch is one sound, a digraph, and it comes before the vowel. So chin is ch + in."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Onsets & Rimes: in the word ship, what is the onset?","choices":["sh","s","shi"],"answer":0,"hint":"The onset is every sound before the vowel. Is sh one sound or two?","explain":"s and h together make one sound, sh. It comes before the vowel i, so the onset is sh and the rime is ip."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You make {n} words in the -ug family, like bug and mug. Each one is a CVC word with 3 sounds. How many sounds do you say in all?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Each word has a first, a middle and a last sound. Count 3 for every word.","explain":"3 sounds in each word, and {n} words, is {=3*n} sounds. The middle and last sounds are the same each time: u, g.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Change the first sound in dog to l. What new word do you make?","choices":["log","dol","lid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Only the first sound changes. Keep o and g the same.","explain":"Swap d for l and keep og: l + og = log."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Every one-syllable word splits into 2 parts: an onset and a rime. You split {n} words. How many parts is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each word gives you 2 parts. Count 2 for each word.","explain":"2 parts for each of {n} words makes {=2*n} parts: {n} onsets and {n} rimes.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Words have parts. In cat, the first part is c. That is the onset. The rest is at. That is the rime.","Push them together and say it fast: c, at, cat! That is blending. Now swap the onset. h, at, hat. m, at, mat. Same rime, new word.","The rime starts at the vowel. In ship, s and h make one sound, sh. So ship is sh and ip. In frog, f and r go first. So frog is fr and og.","Say dog slowly: d, o, g. d is the first sound, o is the middle, g is the last. Change one sound and you get a new word. Change d to l and dog is log."],"adult":["This is phonics and phonological awareness for ages 4 to 7. In England it sits in the Reception and Year 1 word reading work: children blend phonemes to read words and segment them to spell. In the US, Common Core RF.K.2 asks children to blend and segment onsets and rimes of one-syllable words, isolate the initial, medial vowel and final phonemes in CVC words, and add or substitute phonemes to make new words.","Key terms: a phoneme is one sound; a grapheme is the letter or letters that write it. The onset is the consonant sound or sounds before the vowel; the rime is the vowel and what follows. A digraph such as sh or ch is two letters for one phoneme, so it is one onset sound. A blend such as fr or st is two phonemes said quickly, and both belong to the onset. Words that share a rime, like bug, mug and hug, form a word family.","Common mistakes: putting the vowel in the onset (do + g), splitting a digraph (s + hip), saying the letter name instead of the sound, and adding an uh sound to stop sounds (cuh-at), which makes blending harder. Be careful with the middle sound: children often say the last sound they heard. Ask them to say the word slowly and tap a finger for each sound.","In the lab your child pushes an onset tile onto a rime and picks the matching picture, builds bug, mug and hug from pictures, cuts dog, ship and frog into onset and rime, taps the first, middle or last sound in a CVC word, and swaps one letter to make bat, cap and pen. Vowel tiles are pink with a dot so the start of the rime is easy to see."]},"familyTip":"Play I spy with rimes: 'I spy something that ends in -at… it starts with h.' Then swap: 'Now change the h to c. What is it?' Keep the sounds short: say c, not cuh.","keyIdea":"A one-syllable word splits into an onset and a rime. The onset is the sound or sounds before the vowel, and the rime is the vowel and everything after it: c + at = cat, sh + ip = ship, fr + og = frog. Blending puts them together to say the word, and segmenting splits it apart. In a CVC word each letter is one sound, so you can find the first, middle and last sound, and change just one sound to make a new word, like cat to bat.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which word family? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["The -at family","The -ig family"],"items":[["cat",0],["pig",1],["hat",0],["wig",1],["mat",0],["dig",1],["sat",0],["big",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrime","caption":"Flagship lab: blend onset and rime, build a word family, cut words at the vowel, find first, middle and last sounds, and swap one sound to make a new word.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_F978c32kDr":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The letter c is called see. What sound does c make at the start of cat?","choices":["k","see","sss"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say cat slowly. Is the first sound like see, or like k?","explain":"In cat, c says k. See is the name of the letter, not its sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the best way to say the sound of m?","choices":["mmm","muh","em"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the sound pure. Hum it with your lips shut.","explain":"m says mmm. Muh adds an extra sound, and em is the letter's name."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which word has a short a sound, like apple?","choices":["cat","cot","cut"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word slowly and listen to the middle sound.","explain":"Cat has a in the middle, like apple. Cot has o, and cut has u."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In bed, what is the middle sound?","choices":["e, like egg","i, like insect","a, like apple"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say b-e-d slowly. e and i sound close, so listen carefully.","explain":"Bed has a short e in the middle, like egg. With i it would be bid."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"In the word go, what does the o say?","choices":["oh, its name","o, like octopus","g"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say go. Does the o sound like octopus, or like the letter's own name?","explain":"In go, the o says its name, oh. That is a long vowel. In dog, the o is short."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which letter makes the first sound in dog?","choices":["d","b","g"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say dog slowly. b and d look alike, so check which way the letter faces.","explain":"Dog starts with d. b faces the other way and says b, like bus. g is at the end of dog."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You flip {n} letter tiles, and each tile shows one sound. How many sounds do you see?","answer":"n","hint":"One tile shows one sound. Count the tiles.","explain":"Each single letter tile shows one sound, so {n} tiles show {n} sounds.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which word starts with the same sound as fish?","choices":["fan","van","pan"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fish starts with fff. Say each word and listen to its first sound.","explain":"Fan starts with fff, like fish. Van starts with vvv, which is close but buzzes, and pan starts with p."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Sam knows {n} letter sounds. This week he learns the sounds for s, a, t and p. How many letter sounds does Sam know now?","answer":"n+4","hint":"Count the new letters: s, a, t, p. Then start at {n} and count on that many.","explain":"s, a, t and p are 4 new sounds. {n} and 4 more make {=n+4} letter sounds.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Single letter sounds: the letter s is called ess. What sound does it make at the start of sun?","choices":["sss","ess","suh"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say sun slowly. Listen to the very first sound. Keep it pure, with no uh.","explain":"s says sss, like a snake. Ess is its name, and suh adds an extra uh sound."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The word cat has 3 letters, and each letter makes one sound. How many sounds are in {n} words like cat?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Each word like cat has 3 sounds: k, a, t. Count 3 for each word.","explain":"{n} words with 3 sounds each make {=3*n} sounds. In cat, c says k, a says a, t says t.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which picture word starts with the sound b?","choices":["bus","dog","pig"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word. Which one starts with a short b sound? Watch out: d faces the other way.","explain":"Bus starts with b. Dog starts with d, and pig starts with p, which is a puff of air."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You know {n} letter sounds. Today you learn 2 more. How many letter sounds do you know now?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 2 more.","explain":"{n} and 2 more make {=n+2} letter sounds. A few new sounds each week adds up.","params":{"n":[3,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Every letter has a name. This is m. Its name is em. But in a word, m says mmm. Hum it: mmm, like moon.","To read, we use the sound, not the name. c is called see. But in cat it says k. So cat is k, a, t.","Keep each sound short. Say mmm, not muh. Say sss, not suh. Then the sounds join up to make a word.","a, e, i, o and u are vowels. They have short sounds: a like apple, e like egg, i like insect, o like octopus, u like umbrella. A vowel can also say its name. In go, o says oh."],"adult":["This is grapheme-phoneme correspondence, the first step of systematic synthetic phonics. A grapheme is a written letter or group of letters; a phoneme is a single speech sound. Single letter sounds match one letter to its most common phoneme. The EYFS early learning goal asks children to say a sound for each letter of the alphabet, CCSS RF.K.3a asks for the primary sound of each consonant, and RF.K.3b asks for the short and long sounds of the five major vowels. In England, Year 1 children should respond speedily with the correct sound to each grapheme.","Key words: letter name, letter sound (phoneme), grapheme, consonant, vowel, short vowel, long vowel, initial sound, medial sound and CVC word (consonant, vowel, consonant, like cat or bed). A long vowel says its letter name: o in go, e in me, i in hi.","Common mistakes: giving the letter name instead of the sound (see for c), adding an uh to sounds (muh, suh, tuh), which makes blending hard because m-a-t becomes muh-a-tuh; mixing up b and d, which face different ways; and mixing up short e and short i, which sound close in bed and bid. Be careful to stretch continuous sounds (mmm, sss, fff) and keep bouncy ones (b, d, t) short and crisp.","In the lab your child flips letter tiles to see each sound and a picture, drags pictures to the letter that makes their first sound, fills in the missing middle vowel of a CVC word, sorts words by whether the vowel is short or says its name, and plays a quick-sounds round. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Play I spy with sounds, not names: 'I spy something that starts with mmm.' Keep the sound pure. When your child guesses, say the word slowly together, mmm-oo-n, and check the first sound.","keyIdea":"Each letter has a name and a sound. The name of m is em, but the sound it makes in a word is mmm. When you read, you use the sound. Say sounds short and pure, mmm not muh. The five vowels a, e, i, o and u have a short sound, like a in cat, and they can also say their name, like o in go.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which sound does it start with? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["sss, like sun","mmm, like moon","t, like tent"],"items":[["sock",0],["map",1],["top",2],["six",0],["milk",1],["tap",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagphon","caption":"Flagship lab: flip letter tiles, match pictures to first sounds, fill the middle vowel and sort short and long vowels.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt__KHQttMde3":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Blend the sounds s-u-n. Which word do they make?","choices":["sun","nus","sit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with the first sound on the left. Say s, then u, then n, and push them together.","explain":"s-u-n makes sun. Nus reads the sounds backwards, and sit has different sounds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How many sounds are in fish?","choices":["3: f-i-sh","4: f-i-s-h","2: f-sh"],"answer":0,"hint":"s and h team up. Count sounds, not letters.","explain":"Fish has 3 sounds: f, i, sh. The s and h make one sound, and the i must not be skipped."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Tom sees a picture of a pen. Which word should he pick?","choices":["pen","pin","pan"],"answer":0,"hint":"All three words start with p and end with n. Read the middle sound.","explain":"Pen has e in the middle, like egg. Pin has i and pan has a, so read every sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Blend the sounds f-r-o-g. Which word do they make?","choices":["frog","fog","for"],"answer":0,"hint":"f and r sit side by side. Say both of them.","explain":"f-r-o-g makes frog. Fog drops the r, and for has different sounds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ava says c, a, t and then stops. What should she do next?","choices":["Push the sounds together: cat","Say the letter names","Guess from the picture"],"answer":0,"hint":"Saying the sounds is the first half of reading the word.","explain":"After saying the sounds, she blends them: c-a-t, cat. Letter names and guessing will not read the word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which two letters make one sound in chop?","choices":["ch","op","co"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a letter team at the start, like sh in ship.","explain":"c and h team up to make ch. So chop has 3 sounds: ch, o, p."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} words each have 3 sounds, like map. Each sound gets one sound button. How many sound buttons do you draw in all?","answer":"3*n","hint":"One sound button for each sound. Each word has 3 sounds.","explain":"{n} words with 3 sounds each need {=3*n} sound buttons: one dot for each sound.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In stop, which two consonants sit side by side at the start?","choices":["s and t","s and o","t and p"],"answer":0,"hint":"Consonants are the letters that are not a, e, i, o or u. Look at the first two letters.","explain":"s and t sit side by side, so you say both: s-t-o-p. o is a vowel, and t and p are not next to each other."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Chick has 5 letters but only 3 sounds, because ch and ck are letter teams. A word has {n} letters and two letter teams. How many sounds does it have?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Each team of two letters makes one sound. Two teams means take 2 away.","explain":"Two letter teams save 2 sounds, so {n} letters make {=n-2} sounds. Chick: ch, i, ck.","params":{"n":[4,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Blend the sounds c-u-p. Which word do they make?","choices":["cup","cap","cut"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each sound in order, then push them together. Listen to the middle and the last sound.","explain":"c-u-p blends to cup. Cap has a in the middle, and cut ends with t."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. Two of them team up to make one sound, like sh. How many sounds does the word have?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Each letter makes one sound, but the team of two makes only one sound. So take 1 away.","explain":"The two team letters share one sound, so {n} letters make {=n-1} sounds. Ship has 4 letters and 3 sounds.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is the best way to say the sounds to read map?","choices":["mmm-a-p","muh-a-puh","em-ay-pee"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep each sound short and pure. Do not use the letter names.","explain":"mmm-a-p blends easily into map. Muh-a-puh adds extra uh sounds, and em-ay-pee are the letter names."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Frog has 4 sounds: f-r-o-g. You blend {n} words like frog. How many sounds do you say in all?","answer":"4*n","hint":"Each word like frog has 4 sounds. Count 4 for each word.","explain":"{n} words with 4 sounds each make {=4*n} sounds. Remember to say both f and r in frog.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You can read a word you have never seen. Look at the letters. Say the sound for each one, from left to right. Then push the sounds together. m, a, p. Map!","Keep each sound short. Say mmm, not muh. Say t, not tuh. Extra uh sounds get in the way. Muh-a-puh does not sound like map.","Some letters team up. s and h make one sound: sh. So ship is sh, i, p. It has 4 letters but 3 sounds. c and h make ch, and c and k make one k sound, like in chick.","Look at every letter, not just the first one. Big and bag both start with b. The middle sound tells them apart. In frog, say f and r. Do not skip one."],"adult":["Blending is the core decoding skill in systematic synthetic phonics. The child says the phoneme for each grapheme in order, left to right, then merges the phonemes into a spoken word. The English national curriculum asks Year 1 pupils to respond speedily with the correct sound to graphemes for all 40+ phonemes and to read unfamiliar words by blending the sounds of taught grapheme-phoneme correspondences (GPCs). Common Core RF.K.3 and RF.1.3b ask children to decode regularly spelled one-syllable words, and RF.1.2b asks them to blend sounds orally, including consonant blends.","Key words: phoneme (one speech sound), grapheme (the letter or letters for one sound), digraph (two letters, one sound: sh, ch, th, ck), sound buttons (a dot under a one-letter grapheme and a line under a digraph), CVC word (consonant, vowel, consonant, like map), and adjacent consonants, often called a consonant blend: the f and r in frog make a CCVC word.","Common mistakes: adding uh to each sound (muh-a-puh), which hides the word; saying the sounds but never merging them; reading the two letters of a digraph apart (s-h-i-p); dropping one of two adjacent consonants, so frog is read as fog; and guessing from the first letter or the picture, so bag is read as big. Be careful to hold stretchy sounds (mmm, sss, fff) and clip bouncy ones (p, t, b) short. If blending is hard, practise it out loud first with no print at all.","In the lab your child picks the pure way to say each sound, drags a car under the sound buttons so each sound lights up and then merges into the word, taps letters to join the digraph teams, matches a picture to one of three words that differ by one sound, and blends four-sound words with adjacent consonants. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Play robot talk. Say a word in sounds, like c-u-p, and let your child shout the word. Then swap: point at a cup and let them say it in robot sounds. Keep each sound short, with no uh on the end.","keyIdea":"To read a new word, say the sound for each letter from left to right, then push the sounds together: m-a-p, map. This is called blending. Keep each sound short and pure, mmm not muh. Two letters can make one sound, like sh in ship, so ship has 4 letters but 3 sounds. When two consonants sit side by side, as in frog, say both. Read every sound, not just the first, so you can tell bag from big.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Count the sounds, not the letters. Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["3 sounds","4 sounds"],"items":[["ship",0],["frog",1],["chick",0],["stop",1],["fish",0],["crab",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagblend","caption":"Flagship lab: say pure sounds, drive the blending car, join digraph teams, read every sound and blend longer words.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ZhvwM6LMBL":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Leo reads very, very fast. He runs past every full stop. What goes wrong?","choices":["It stops making sense","Nothing, fast is best","His voice goes up too much"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the full stops are for.","explain":"Racing past the full stops runs the ideas together, so it stops making sense. Fluent reading is talking speed, not a race."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which book is just right to read by yourself?","choices":["I can read 9 of 10 words","I can read 6 of 10 words","I can read 4 of 10 words"],"answer":0,"hint":"Just right means only a few words need sounding out.","explain":"9 of 10 words is just right. With 6 or 4 of 10 there are too many stops, so read those books with a grown-up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"We won the race! How do you read this line?","choices":["With a strong, happy voice","In a quiet, flat voice","Like asking a question"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the mark at the end of the line.","explain":"An exclamation mark asks for a strong voice that shows the feeling. A question would end with a question mark."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the best way to get smoother at reading a book?","choices":["Read it again and again","Read it once and stop","Look at the pictures and guess"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what happens to the stops each time you read the same page.","explain":"Rereading the same book makes the words quicker each time, so the reading gets smoother. Guessing from pictures skips the real words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where does the first scoop end in: The little bird sang in the tree.","choices":["After bird","After The","After sang in"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: who? Then ask: what did it do?","explain":"Who? The little bird. What did it do? Sang in the tree. So the first scoop ends after bird."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Zoe reads: The… dog… is… wet. What can she try?","choices":["Read it in scoops: The dog… is wet.","Read it faster with no stops","Say each word louder"],"answer":0,"hint":"One word at a time sounds like a robot. Group the words.","explain":"Reading in scoops, The dog… is wet, sounds like talking. Going faster or louder does not group the words."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A line has {n} scoops. Each scoop has 3 words. How many words are in the line?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Count 3 words for each scoop.","explain":"{n} scoops of 3 words make {=3*n} words. You read each scoop in one go.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam reads a page of 10 words and can read {n} of them. How many does he get stuck on?","answer":"10-n","hint":"Take the words he can read away from 10.","explain":"10 take away {n} is {=10-n}. If he gets stuck on more than 1 word in 10, read the book together.","params":{"n":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Is the cat asleep? and The cat is asleep. have the same words. How do you read them?","choices":["Differently: the question goes up","The same, because the words match","Both loud and strong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the two end marks.","explain":"The end mark changes the voice. The question goes up, and the full stop line is calm."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which way of reading sounds most like talking?","choices":["My cat… sat on the mat.","My… cat… sat… on… the… mat.","Mycatsatonthemat."],"answer":0,"hint":"Talking puts words that go together into one group, a scoop.","explain":"My cat… sat on the mat reads in two scoops, like talking. One word at a time sounds like a robot, and running it all together is too fast to follow."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A page has 10 words. You get stuck on {n} of them. How many words can you read?","answer":"10-n","hint":"Take the words you got stuck on away from 10.","explain":"10 take away {n} is {=10-n}. If that is 9 or 10, the book is just right for reading by yourself.","params":{"n":[0,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A line ends with a question mark: Where is my hat? How should your voice go?","choices":["Up at the end, like asking","Flat, the same as every line","Loud and strong, like shouting"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the mark at the end of the line. What does it tell you?","explain":"A question mark means you are asking, so your voice goes up. A strong voice is for an exclamation mark, and a flat voice hides the question."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia reads a page and gets stuck {n} times. Each time she reads it again, she gets stuck 1 time fewer. How many times does she get stuck on her next read?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take 1 away from the number of times she got stuck.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}. Each reread makes the words easier, so the stops go down.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Fluent reading sounds like talking. A robot reads like this: the… big… dog… ran. You can read in scoops. The big dog… ran to the park. Each scoop is a group of words that go together.","Look at the mark at the end. A full stop says: stop, and be calm. A question mark says: you are asking, so let your voice go up. An exclamation mark says: use a strong voice!","Fast is not the best. Too fast and you run past the stops, and it does not make sense. Too slow and you forget the start. Read at talking speed.","Read the same book again. The first time, you may get stuck and sound out a word. The next time it is quicker. Pick books where you can read 9 or 10 words out of 10."],"adult":["Fluency means reading the words correctly, at a natural pace, with expression. Experts call the expression part prosody. When reading is fluent, your child has more attention left for meaning. The English national curriculum asks Year 1 pupils to read aloud books that match the phonics they know, and to reread them to build fluency and confidence. In Year 2 they read such books accurately, automatically and without undue hesitation. Common Core RF.K.4 asks for emergent-reader texts read with purpose and understanding. RF.1.4b asks for reading aloud with accuracy, appropriate rate and expression on successive readings.","Key words: accuracy (words read correctly), automaticity (knowing a word at once, with no slow sounding out), rate (how many words are read correctly in a minute), prosody (phrasing, stress and the rise and fall of the voice), phrasing (reading in groups of words that belong together, often called scooping), repeated reading (reading the same text a few times) and a decodable book (one that uses only the phonics your child has been taught). This topic asks for reading with at least 90% accuracy, about 9 words in 10. Many teachers aim higher, about 19 words in 20, for reading alone. Below 9 in 10, read the book together. The DfE reading framework adds that a decodable book should match the sounds already taught, so every word can be sounded out.","Common mistakes: treating speed as the goal, so children race past full stops and lose the meaning; robot reading, one word at a time; a flat voice that ignores question and exclamation marks; books that are too hard, so every line stops for sounding out; reading a book once and moving on; and guessing words from the picture. Be careful not to time children with a stopwatch at this age. Praise smooth rereading with a voice that fits.","In the lab your child taps where the first scoop of a line ends. They drag a calm, asking or excited voice onto lines that end in a full stop, question mark or exclamation mark. They read one page three times and see the stops fall from 3 to 1 to none. They sort four books by how many words in 10 they can read. Last, they drag a speed dial to hear why snail and rocket reading both lose the meaning. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Pick a short book your child knows well. Read one page to them like a storyteller, then let them read the same page back to you. Read it again tomorrow. Point out one thing that got better, like a smooth scoop or a question that went up.","keyIdea":"Fluent reading sounds like talking. You read the words right, at talking speed, with a voice that fits. Read in scoops, groups of words that go together, not one word at a time. Let the end mark set your voice: calm for a full stop, up for a question mark, strong for an exclamation mark. Pick a book where you can read 9 or 10 words out of 10, and read it again and again. Each time, the stops get fewer and the reading gets smoother.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fluent or not yet? Drag each way of reading, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fluent","Not yet"],"items":[["Reads in scoops, like talking",0],["Reads one… word… at… a… time",1],["Voice goes up at a question mark",0],["Races past every full stop",1],["Reads the book again to get smoother",0],["Gets stuck on 5 words in 10",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfluent","caption":"Flagship lab: scoop words into phrases, set the voice from the end mark, reread a page, pick a just right book and find talking speed.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9-OHslmt1g":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many sounds are in chip?","choices":["3: ch-i-p","4: c-h-i-p","2: ch-p"],"answer":0,"hint":"c and h are a team. Count sounds, not letters.","explain":"Chip has 3 sounds: ch, i, p. The c and h make one sound, and the i must not be skipped."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word is spelled right?","choices":["duck","duk","duc"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen to the vowel just before the k sound. Is it short?","explain":"Duck has a short u just before the k sound, so it ends in ck."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which word starts with sh?","choices":["sheep","sun","cheese"],"answer":0,"hint":"sh sounds like a quiet finger on your lips: shh.","explain":"Sheep starts with sh. Sun starts with s on its own, and cheese starts with ch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What sound does wh make in wheel?","choices":["w","h","wuh-huh"],"answer":0,"hint":"w and h are a team. Say wheel out loud.","explain":"wh says w, so wheel starts with a w sound. The two letters make one sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A little stick that lights a fire. Which spelling is right?","choices":["match","mach","matsh"],"answer":0,"hint":"The a in match is short. What comes after a short vowel?","explain":"After a short vowel, the ch sound is spelled tch: match, catch, fetch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word ends with ng?","choices":["ring","rig","rich"],"answer":0,"hint":"ng is the sound at the end of sing.","explain":"Ring ends with ng, one sound. Rig ends with g, and rich ends with ch."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Chick has 5 letters but only 3 sounds, because ch and ck are teams. A word has {n} letters and two teams. How many sounds does it have?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Each team of two letters makes one sound. Two teams means take 2 away.","explain":"Two teams save 2 sounds, so {n} letters make {=n-2} sounds. Chick: ch, i, ck.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Mia draws one line under each team of two letters. She draws {n} lines for words like sh, ch and th. How many letters are in her teams?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each team has 2 letters. Count 2 for each line.","explain":"{n} teams of 2 letters make {=2*n} letters, but only {n} sounds.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which word ends in just k, not ck?","choices":["milk","duck","sock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the letter just before the k sound. Is it a vowel or a consonant?","explain":"In milk, l comes just before the k sound, so it is just k. Duck and sock have a short vowel before, so they use ck."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which two letters make one sound in fish?","choices":["sh","fi","is"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say fish slowly. Listen for the sound at the end.","explain":"s and h team up to say sh. Fish has 3 sounds: f, i, sh."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. One pair of letters is a team, like ck in duck. How many sounds does the word have?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Each letter makes a sound, but the team of two makes only one. Take 1 away.","explain":"The two team letters share one sound, so {n} letters make {=n-1} sounds. Duck has 4 letters and 3 sounds.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What sound does ph make in dolphin?","choices":["f","p","h"],"answer":0,"hint":"p and h are a team. Say dolphin out loud.","explain":"ph says f, so dolphin sounds like dol-fin. It does not say p."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ship has 3 sounds: sh-i-p. You read {n} words like ship. How many sounds do you say in all?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Each word like ship has 3 sounds. Count 3 for each word.","explain":"{n} words with 3 sounds each make {=3*n} sounds. The sh counts as one sound each time.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some letters work as a team. s and h stick together and say sh. Ship is sh, i, p. One team, one sound.","Teams to know: sh in ship, ch in chip, th in bath, ng in ring and ck in duck. Ship and chip look the same at the end. The team at the start makes them two words.","Two teams play a trick. p and h say f, like in photo. w and h say w, like in wheel. Count sounds, not letters. Duck has 4 letters. Say it slowly: d, u, ck. That is 3 sounds.","Duck ends in ck, and match ends in tch. That is because a short vowel sound comes just before. Milk ends in k, as l comes just before."],"adult":["A consonant digraph is two letters that spell one consonant phoneme. Year 1 of the English national curriculum teaches ch, sh, th, ng and ck, the /k/ sound spelt ck after a single short vowel, tch for /tʃ/ after a single short vowel (with exceptions such as rich, which, much and such), ph and wh in words like dolphin and wheel, and k rather than c before e, i and y (kit, skip). Common Core RF.1.3a asks children to know the spelling-sound links for common consonant digraphs.","Key words: phoneme (one sound), grapheme (the letter or letters that spell it), digraph (two letters, one sound), trigraph (three letters, one sound, such as tch), and sound buttons: a dot under a single letter and a line under a digraph. Children who segment by sound can spell; those who count letters cannot.","Common mistakes: reading the two letters apart (s-h-i-p), which breaks the word; counting letters instead of sounds; reading ph as p; hearing sh in any word that starts with s, such as snake; and writing duk for duck or mach for match. Be careful with th: it has a quiet sound in thin and a buzzy sound in that, but it is the same digraph.","In the lab your child drags sh, ch or th into a word and lets the picture decide (ship or chip), counts sounds in duck, ring and chick, sorts ph and wh words by the sound they make, chooses ck or k and tch or ch to finish a word, and hunts the pictures that start with sh. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Go on a sound hunt at home. Find three things that start with sh, ch or th: a shoe, a chair, a thumb. Say each one slowly and ask: is it one sound or two at the start?","keyIdea":"A consonant digraph is two letters that make one sound: sh in ship, ch in chip, th in bath, ng in ring and ck in duck. Count sounds, not letters, so duck has 4 letters but 3 sounds. ph says f, as in photo, and wh says w, as in wheel. At the end of a short word, straight after a short vowel, the k sound is spelled ck (duck) and the ch sound is spelled tch (match). After a consonant it is just k or ch: milk, lunch.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which team starts the word? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["sh","ch","th"],"items":[["shop",0],["chin",1],["thin",2],["shed",0],["chop",1],["that",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdgraph","caption":"Flagship lab: drag digraph magnets into words, count sounds not letters, sort ph and wh, pick ck or tch endings and hunt for sh.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_t1JXeNgKcu":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does can't mean?","choices":["cannot","can it","can too"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word has the letters c, a, n, n, o, t?","explain":"Can't is short for cannot. The n and o drop out and the apostrophe goes in their place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What does it's mean in: It's raining?","choices":["it is","it was","its"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try saying it is in its place. Does the sentence still make sense?","explain":"It's is it is: the i of is drops out. It is raining makes sense."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What does the apostrophe in we'll show?","choices":["Letters are missing","The word has ended","Someone owns it"],"answer":0,"hint":"We'll is short for two words. What happened to some letters?","explain":"We'll is we will. The w and i drop out and the apostrophe shows where."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the short way to say they are?","choices":["they're","theyare","the'yre"],"answer":0,"hint":"The a of are drops out. Put the apostrophe where it was.","explain":"They are becomes they're. The apostrophe takes the place of the a."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word is a contraction? We're going home.","choices":["We're","going","home"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the word with the little mark in it.","explain":"We're has an apostrophe. It is short for we are."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A story has {a} contractions on page 1 and {b} on page 2. How many contractions are in the story?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the contractions on both pages.","explain":"{a} and {b} make {=a+b} contractions. Each one hides two words.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam writes I'm {n} times. Each time the a of am drops out. How many letters did he leave out in all?","answer":"n","hint":"One letter goes each time. How many times did he write I'm?","explain":"One a drops out each time, so {n} times leaves out {n} letters.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is right: I'm or I'am?","choices":["I'm","I'am","Both are right"],"answer":0,"hint":"The apostrophe takes the place of a letter. Should the letter stay too?","explain":"I'm is right. The apostrophe replaces the a, so the a does not stay."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does I'm mean?","choices":["I am","I will","I can"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the two long words. Which letter dropped out of I am?","explain":"I'm is I am squashed together. The apostrophe sits where the a of am was."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Tom reads {a} words with an apostrophe for missing letters, like don't. He also reads {b} words with no apostrophe. How many contractions does he read?","answer":"a","hint":"A contraction has an apostrophe for missing letters. Count only those words.","explain":"Only the {a} words with an apostrophe are contractions. The {b} other words are not.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which one has the apostrophe in the right place?","choices":["don't","do'nt","dont'"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do not loses the o from not. Where was that o?","explain":"Don't puts the apostrophe where the o of not was, between the n and the t."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Kim writes {n} sentences. In each one she squashes do not into don't. How many apostrophes does she write?","answer":"n","hint":"Each don't has one apostrophe. How many don'ts are there?","explain":"Each don't needs one apostrophe, so {n} sentences need {n} apostrophes.","params":{"n":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Sometimes we squash two words into one. I am turns into I'm. It is quicker to say.","When the words squash, some letters drop out. A little mark goes in the gap. It is called an apostrophe.","The apostrophe sits right where the letters went. In don't, it sits where the o of not was.","When you read a short word like can't, think of the long words. Can't means cannot. It's means it is."],"adult":["In England, Year 1 word reading asks children to read words with contractions, such as I'm, I'll and we'll. They should know that the apostrophe stands for the letter or letters left out. In Year 2 they learn to spell these words with the apostrophe in the right place, so reading them well now helps with writing next year.","The words to use: a contraction is a short form of two words. The apostrophe of omission marks where letters have been left out. Not is the most common partner (don't, can't, didn't, isn't), along with am, is, are, will and have (I'm, she's, they're, we'll, I've). Can't comes from cannot, which is already one word, so only the n and o drop.","Common mistakes: putting the apostrophe between the two words (do'nt, not don't); leaving it out (Im, dont); keeping the dropped letter too (I'am); and mixing up we'll with well, or it's with its. Be careful not to teach it's and its with rules about owning yet. At this age the simple test is: can you say it is in its place?","In the lab your child taps out the letters that drop and drags the apostrophe into the gap. Then they unzip short words to their long forms, pick the right gap in dont, shes and theyre, spot three contractions in a tiny story, and squash two words in a sentence into one. Every drag also has a big button."]},"familyTip":"When you read together, stop at a word like don't or I'm. Ask: which two words are hiding in here? Cover the apostrophe with a finger and ask which letters went missing.","keyIdea":"A contraction is two words squashed into one short word. Some letters drop out, and an apostrophe ( ' ) goes in their place. I am becomes I'm, do not becomes don't, and we will becomes we'll. To read a contraction, say the two long words in your head: can't means cannot, it's means it is.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Contraction or not? Drag each card to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Contraction","No apostrophe"],"items":[["don't",0],["dog",1],["I'm",0],["jam",1],["we'll",0],["well",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcontr","caption":"Flagship lab: squash two words into one, unzip short words, find the apostrophe's gap, spot contractions in a story.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_BtMbZibZUj":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word has the same oo sound as book?","choices":["foot","moon","spoon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say book. Is the oo short or long? Find the word with the short one.","explain":"book and foot both have the short oo. moon and spoon have the long oo."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word is spelt the right way?","choices":["boy","boi","bo"],"answer":0,"hint":"The sound is at the end of the word. Which spelling goes at the end?","explain":"oy usually goes at the end of a word: boy, toy. oi goes in the middle: coin."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which two spellings can both make the ee sound?","choices":["ee and ea","ee and oa","ai and oi"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of tree and sea. Which letters make the ee sound in each?","explain":"ee in tree and ea in sea make the same sound. Some sounds have two spellings."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In the word coin, which letters make one sound together?","choices":["oi","co","in"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say coin slowly: c… then the middle sound… then n.","explain":"o and i make one sound, oi: c-oi-n. That is the vowel digraph."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How many boxes does seed need in a phoneme frame?","choices":["3 boxes","4 boxes","2 boxes"],"answer":0,"hint":"One box for each sound, not each letter. Say s-ee-d.","explain":"seed has 3 sounds, s-ee-d, so it needs 3 boxes. ee shares one box."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word has a long oo, like moon?","choices":["food","good","look"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word and stretch the oo. Which sounds like moon?","explain":"food has the long oo, like moon. good and look have the short oo, like book."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia reads {n} words with ai and {k} words with ay. How many words with the ai sound did she read?","answer":"n+k","hint":"ai and ay both make the same sound. Add the two groups.","explain":"{n} words with ai and {k} with ay make {=n+k} words, all with the same ai sound.","params":{"n":[1,5],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which word has the same sound as toe?","choices":["boat","boot","bat"],"answer":0,"hint":"oe in toe says oa. Which word has oa?","explain":"oe in toe and oa in boat make the same sound. boot has oo and bat has a short a."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A word has {n} letters and one vowel digraph. A phoneme frame has one box for each sound. How many boxes does the word need?","answer":"n-1","hint":"The two letters of the digraph share one box. Every other letter gets its own box.","explain":"{n} letters, but the digraph's two letters share a box, so the frame needs {=n-1} boxes, like c-oi-n in 3 boxes.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many sounds are in the word boat?","choices":["3: b-oa-t","4: b-o-a-t","2: b-oat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the two letters that make one sound, then count sounds, not letters.","explain":"oa is a vowel digraph, so it is one sound: b-oa-t. That is 3 sounds from 4 letters."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. Two of them make one sound together. Every other letter makes its own sound. How many sounds?","answer":"n-1","hint":"The two letters that work together count as just one sound.","explain":"{n} letters, but two of them share one sound, so the word has {=n-1} sounds. Rain: 4 letters, 3 sounds.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which spelling is right for the end of the word d_ (day)?","choices":["ay","ai","a"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the gap in the middle of the word or at the end?","explain":"At the end of a word we usually write ay: day, play, say. ai goes in the middle: rain."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam writes a word with {n} sounds. One of the sounds is a vowel digraph. How many letters does he write?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Each sound is one letter, except the digraph, which uses two.","explain":"{n} sounds need {n} graphemes. The digraph uses 2 letters, so there are {=n+1} letters, like s-ee-d.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some sounds need two letters. In rain, a and i make one sound: ai. Two letters, one sound.","So rain has 4 letters but 3 sounds: r-ai-n. Tree is t-r-ee. Boat is b-oa-t.","Where is the sound? In the middle we write ai or oi: rain, coin. At the end we write ay or oy: play, boy.","Listen to oo. In moon it is long. In book it is short. Same letters, two sounds."],"adult":["In England, phonics in Year 1 (National Curriculum 2014, Word Reading) asks children to respond speedily with the correct sound to graphemes for all 40+ phonemes, including alternative sounds for graphemes, and to read words containing taught GPCs. The Year 1 spelling appendix lists the vowel digraphs ai, oi, ay, oy, ee, ea, oa, oe and oo, and notes that ai and oi are virtually never used at the end of English words while ay and oy are used there. In the US, Common Core RF.1.3.c asks children to know final -e and common vowel team conventions for long vowel sounds.","Words to use: a grapheme is the letter or letters that write one sound (phoneme). A digraph is a grapheme of two letters; a vowel digraph (a vowel team) makes a vowel sound. Sound buttons show this: a dot under each single-letter grapheme and a line under a digraph. A phoneme frame has one box per sound, so coin needs 3 boxes, with oi sharing a box.","Common mistakes: sounding every letter (b-o-a-t, which gives four sounds and no word); spelling the end of play as plai; reading every oo as the long sound (so foot rhymes with boot); and thinking ee and ea must sound different. Be careful with ea: it usually says ee (sea, dream) but in some words it says a short e (head, bread), so check the word makes sense.","In the lab your child joins the two letters that make one sound and watches the sound buttons change, builds coin, seed and moon in phoneme frames, chooses ai or ay and oi or oy by where the gap sits, sorts oo words into moon or book, and posts words into sound houses for ee, ai and oa. Every drag has a button twin."]},"familyTip":"Write a word like rain or boat on paper. Ask your child to draw a dot under each letter that makes its own sound and a line under the two letters that work together. Then count the sounds on fingers: r-ai-n, 3 sounds.","keyIdea":"A vowel digraph is two letters that make one vowel sound: ai in rain, ee in tree, oa in boat, oi in coin. Count sounds, not letters: r-ai-n has 4 letters but 3 sounds. Some sounds have two spellings: ai in the middle of a word, ay at the end (rain, play); oi in the middle, oy at the end (coin, boy). And one spelling can make two sounds: oo is long in moon and short in book.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which sound? Drag each word to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["says ee","says oa"],"items":[["tree",0],["boat",1],["sea",0],["toe",1],["feet",0],["road",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagvowel","caption":"Flagship lab: join two letters into one sound, fill phoneme frames, pick ai or ay by position, sort long and short oo, post words to sound houses.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_OgJPbGkrYk":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word has the j sound spelled g?","choices":["giraffe","jelly","goat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word. Which one starts with j but is spelled with g?","explain":"Giraffe starts with the j sound, spelled g. Jelly uses j, and goat has a hard g sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do you read “write”?","choices":["r-i-te, the w is quiet","w-r-i-te, say every letter","w-i-te, the r is quiet"],"answer":0,"hint":"wr at the start of a word makes one sound.","explain":"wr says r, so write sounds just like right. The w is silent."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which ending finishes “bri_” to make bridge?","choices":["dge","ge","j"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the i in bridge short or long?","explain":"The i in bridge is short, so the j sound is spelled dge."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which word has a soft c that says s?","choices":["city","cot","cup"],"answer":0,"hint":"c says s before e, i or y.","explain":"In city, c comes before i, so it says s. In cot and cup, c says k."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mo reads “knock” as k-nock. What should he change?","choices":["Not say the k: kn says n","Say it faster","Say the c as s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which letter in knock makes no sound?","explain":"The k in knock is silent. kn says n, so it is n-o-ck."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word has the zh sound, like the middle of television?","choices":["treasure","sun","ship"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word slowly. Listen for a soft buzzy sound.","explain":"In treasure, s says zh. Sun has the s sound and ship has sh."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A sound house for s has {a} words with s, {b} words with c and {k} words with ce. How many words are in the house?","answer":"a+b+k","hint":"s, c and ce all spell the s sound. Add all three groups.","explain":"{a} + {b} + {k} = {=a+b+k} words, three spellings of one sound.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which ending finishes “ca_” to make cage?","choices":["ge","dge","j"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the a in cage short like in bad, or long like in day?","explain":"The a in cage is long, so the j sound is spelled ge, not dge."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which word starts with gn, where the g is quiet?","choices":["gnome","goat","gem"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the word that starts with the n sound.","explain":"In gnome, gn says n. Goat starts with a hard g and gem with a j sound."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"A word ends in dge and has {n} letters. d, g and e make one sound together. Every other letter makes its own sound. How many sounds?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Three letters, d g e, share one sound. Take away the two extra letters.","explain":"{n} letters, but dge is one sound, so there are {=n-2} sounds. Badge: 5 letters, 3 sounds, b-a-dge.","params":{"n":[4,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word has the n sound spelled kn?","choices":["knee","nest","neck"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a k you cannot hear at the start.","explain":"In knee, the k is silent. kn says n. Nest and neck use the plain n spelling."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ravi finds {a} words with ge and {b} words with dge. All of them have the j sound. How many j words did he find?","answer":"a+b","hint":"ge and dge both spell the j sound. Add the two groups.","explain":"{a} and {b} make {=a+b} words. Two spellings, one sound: j.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What sound does c make in “face”?","choices":["s","k","f"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the letter just after c.","explain":"c comes before e, so it says s. Face sounds like f-ai-s."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A word starts with kn and has {n} letters. kn makes one sound, and every other letter makes its own sound. How many sounds?","answer":"n-1","hint":"k and n share one sound because the k is silent.","explain":"{n} letters, but k and n make one sound, so there are {=n-1} sounds. Knot: 4 letters, 3 sounds, n-o-t.","params":{"n":[3,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You know lots of sounds. Now you will find new ways to write them. The j sound is j in jam. It is g in giraffe, ge in cage and dge in bridge.","Some letters hide. In knot you do not say the k. The k and the n go together and say n. In wrap, the w is quiet, so wr says r.","c can say two sounds. Look at the next letter. If it is e, i or y, c says s, like in city. If not, c says k, like in cat.","At the end of a word, the j sound is dge or ge. Say the vowel. A short sound, like the a in bad, takes dge: badge. A long sound, like the a in day, takes ge: cage."],"adult":["This is the Year 2 phonics step in the English National Curriculum: children learn alternative graphemes for phonemes they already know. It matches the US Common Core standards RF.1.3 and RF.2.3, which ask children to know spelling-sound correspondences and to decode words with common spelling patterns. A phoneme is a sound; a grapheme is the letter or letters that spell it.","The key correspondences here are /dʒ/ as j, g, ge and dge; /s/ as c and ce (soft c before e, i, y); /n/ as kn and gn; /r/ as wr; and /ʒ/ as s in treasure and television. In kn, gn and wr the first letter is silent, but it still belongs to the grapheme. The dge spelling follows a short vowel (badge, hedge, fudge), while ge follows a long vowel or a consonant (cage, huge, large).","Common mistakes: reading every c as k (saying kity for city), sounding the silent letter (k-nock), splitting kn or dge into separate sounds when counting phonemes, and using j at the end of a word (apart from a few borrowed words such as Raj, English words do not end in j). Children may also think a word with an unusual spelling is spelled wrong. Be careful to say the pure sound, not 'juh' or 'nuh'.","In the lab your child posts words into sound houses, taps every letter that makes a sound (including the silent ones), drags dge or ge onto a word by listening to its vowel, and uses the letter after c to choose between k and s."]},"familyTip":"Go on a sound hunt in a book. Pick one sound, such as j, and write each word you find under its spelling: j, g, ge or dge. Ask: which spelling did we find most often? Which word has a quiet letter?","keyIdea":"One phoneme (sound) can be spelled by more than one grapheme (letter or letters). The j sound can be j, g, ge or dge (jam, giraffe, cage, bridge). The s sound can be s, c or ce (sun, city, mice). The n sound can be n, kn or gn (net, knot, gnome), and the r sound can be wr (write). Some spellings have a silent letter. Clues help you choose: c says s before e, i or y, and dge comes after a short vowel sound.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which sound? Drag each word to its sound house, or tap it and then tap a house.","opts":{"bins":["j sound","s sound","n sound"],"items":[["gem",0],["hedge",0],["ice",1],["face",1],["knock",2],["gnat",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagaltsp","caption":"Flagship lab: post words into sound houses, find hidden letters, choose dge or ge, and read soft c.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4GiE83rJF_":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word rhymes with fox?","choices":["box","fish","frog"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say fox, then each word. Listen for the end, ox.","explain":"Fox and box both end in ox. Fish and frog start like fox, but that is not a rhyme."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Cake, snake, kite. Which one does not rhyme?","choices":["kite","cake","snake"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two of them end with ake. Which one does not?","explain":"Cake and snake end in ake. Kite ends in ite, so it is the odd one out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Bear and chair are spelt in different ways. Do they rhyme?","choices":["Yes, they end with the same sound","No, the letters are different","No, they mean different things"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say them out loud. Rhyme is about sound.","explain":"Bear and chair both end with the sound air. They rhyme, even though the letters are different."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which word rhymes with honey?","choices":["money","hammer","hungry"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a long word, listen to the last part: hon-ey.","explain":"Honey and money both end in oney. Hammer and hungry start like honey, but their ends are different."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You say {n} words that rhyme with cat. Then you think of 1 more. How many rhyming words do you have now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count on 1 from the words you already have.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1} words that rhyme with cat.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What makes two words rhyme?","choices":["They end with the same sound","They start with the same sound","They mean the same thing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of cat and hat. What part is the same?","explain":"Rhyming words have the same end sound, like cat and hat. The start can be different."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} goats want to sail away. Which word rhymes with goat?","choices":["boat","goal","gate"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say goat, then each word. Listen for the end, oat.","explain":"Goat and boat both end in oat, so the {n} goats need a boat. Goal sounds close and gate starts the same, but their ends are different.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which pair of words rhymes?","choices":["kitten and mitten","kitten and cat","kitten and kettle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the two words that end the same way.","explain":"Kitten and mitten both end in itten. Cat means a grown-up kitten but does not rhyme, and kettle only starts the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"In a poem, where do the rhymes often go?","choices":["At the ends of the lines","At the start of each line","Only in the title"],"answer":0,"hint":"A big brown bear sat on a chair. Where are bear and chair?","explain":"Rhymes often go at the ends of lines, like bear and chair. That helps you guess the next rhyme."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word rhymes with cat?","choices":["hat","cup","dog"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word with cat. Which one ends with at?","explain":"Hat and cat both end in at, so they rhyme. Cup starts like cat, but its end is different."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You find {n} pairs of rhyming words, like cat and hat. Each pair has 2 words. How many words is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each pair is 2 words. Count 2 for every pair.","explain":"{n} pairs with 2 words in each is {=2*n} words.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Do sun and sock rhyme?","choices":["No, only the start is the same","Yes, they both start with s","Yes, they are both short"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rhyming words end the same. Listen to the ends: s-un, s-ock.","explain":"Sun ends in un and sock ends in ock. Only the start is the same, so they do not rhyme."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} bees sit in a tree. Which word rhymes with bee?","choices":["tree","bus","bell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say bee, then each word. Which one ends with ee?","explain":"Bee and tree both end in ee, so they rhyme. Bus and bell only start like bee.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which word sounds close to bed but does not rhyme with it?","choices":["bell","red","sled"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the word that has the same start but a different end.","explain":"Bell starts like bed, but it ends in ll, not ed. Red and sled do rhyme with bed."}],"explain":{"kid":["Cat and hat rhyme. Say them out loud: c-at, h-at. They end with the same sound, at. Words that rhyme have the same end sound. The start can be different.","A word can start like cat and still not rhyme. Cat and cup both start with c, but cup ends in up. Bed and bell sound close, but bell ends in ll. To rhyme, the whole end has to match.","Rhyme is about sound, not letters. Bear and chair do not look the same. But say them: they both end in air, so they rhyme. Say the words and listen. Do not just look.","Poems and songs have lots of rhymes. Often the rhyme comes at the end of a line: A big brown bear sat on a chair. Long words can rhyme too, when the last part sounds the same: honey and money, kitten and mitten."],"adult":["Ages 4 to 6. This matches CCSS RF.K.2.a, recognize and produce rhyming words. It also matches the National Curriculum in England for Year 1 reading: recognising and joining in with predictable phrases, and learning to appreciate rhymes and poems. In the EYFS, Development Matters asks 3 and 4 year olds to spot and suggest rhymes. Key words: rhyme, rhyming words, end sound, onset, rime, phonological awareness, alliteration, line, poem.","Two words rhyme when they share the rime: the last stressed vowel sound and everything after it. Cat and hat share at. Honey and money share the part from the stressed vowel on, oney. Rhyme is a sound skill, so it is judged by ear. Spelling can differ (bear, chair, where), and words that look alike may not rhyme (cow, low). Hearing rhyme is one of the early steps in phonological awareness, before splitting words into single sounds for phonics.","Common mistakes: hearing words that start the same (cat, cup) as a rhyme, which is alliteration; accepting near misses that share the start and the middle sound (bed, bell); judging by letters, not sound; picking a word with a linked meaning (cat, kitten); and, in longer words, matching the start (honey, hammer) not the end. The lab, the practice and the challenge target each of these.","In the lab your child lets only rhymes for cat onto a train, and car and cup are turned away. They find the odd one out in four sets. They pick the words that rhyme with bed but not bell or web. They find the rhyming words at the ends of the lines of a short poem, and match honey, kitten and flower to their rhymes. Each word is spoken when they tap it, if the device can speak."]},"familyTip":"Play a rhyme game on a walk. Say a word, like tree, and take turns to say words that rhyme: bee, see, key. Silly made-up words count too. When you read a rhyming book together, stop before the last word of a line and let your child say it.","keyIdea":"Two words rhyme when they end with the same sound: cat and hat both end in at. The start can be different. Words that start the same, like cat and cup, do not rhyme, and words that sound close, like bed and bell, do not rhyme either. Rhyme is about sound, not spelling: bear and chair rhyme. In poems and songs, rhymes often come at the ends of lines.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it rhyme with bee? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Rhymes with bee","Does not rhyme"],"items":[["tree",0],["bus",1],["key",0],["bed",1],["three",0],["bean",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrhyme","caption":"Flagship lab: fill the cat train with rhymes, find the odd one out, reject close misses, find rhymes in a poem, match long rhymes.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_70Ys4i1AB1":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word is a compound word?","choices":["playground","button","season"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for two whole words that help the meaning.","explain":"A playground is ground where you play: play + ground. Button and season only hide little words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is a goldfish?","choices":["A fish that is gold","Gold that looks like a fish","Some gold and a fish"],"answer":0,"hint":"The last word tells you what the thing is.","explain":"Fish is the last word, so a goldfish is a kind of fish. Gold tells you what colour it is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Foot + ball. How do you write the new word?","choices":["football","foot ball","fotball"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep every letter and leave no gap.","explain":"Football is foot and ball joined with no gap, and every letter stays."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Rain can start {n} compound words, like raincoat. You find 1 more. How many rain words do you know now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count on 1 from the words you already know.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1} compound words that start with rain.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which ending makes a real compound word with rain?","choices":["drop","sit","pen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each one: rain + drop, rain + sit, rain + pen. Which is a real word?","explain":"A raindrop is a drop of rain. Rainsit and rainpen are not real words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A birdhouse is a house for a bird. What is a houseboat?","choices":["A boat you can live in","A house for boats","A house next to a boat"],"answer":0,"hint":"The last word says what the thing is.","explain":"Boat is the last word, so a houseboat is a boat, one that people live in like a house."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"There are {n} cupcakes on a plate. Which two words make cupcake?","choices":["cup + cake","cu + pcake","cupc + ake"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the two words you know inside it.","explain":"Cupcake is cup + cake: a little cake, often baked in a paper cup.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is a compound word?","choices":["Two whole words joined into one","Any very long word","A word that rhymes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of sunshine. What is it made of?","explain":"A compound word is two whole words joined into one new word, like sun + shine."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which word has little words hiding in it but is NOT a compound?","choices":["target","bedroom","farmyard"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the little words help you know what it means?","explain":"Tar and get hide in target, but a target is not tar or get. Bedroom and farmyard are real compounds."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which two words make sunshine?","choices":["sun + shine","sun + shin","sunsh + ine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find a whole word at the start, then a whole word at the end.","explain":"Sunshine is sun + shine. Both parts are real words, and the sun is what shines."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You write {n} compound words. Each one is made of 2 words. How many small words did you join?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each compound word has 2 small words in it. Count 2 for every compound.","explain":"{n} compound words with 2 small words in each is {=2*n} small words.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ear + ring. Which spelling is right?","choices":["earring","earing","ear ring"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep every letter of ear and every letter of ring, with no gap.","explain":"Earring keeps the r at the end of ear and the r at the start of ring, so it has two r's."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam sees {n} snowmen in the park. Where do you cut the word snowman?","choices":["snow | man","sno | wman","snowm | an"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cut where both parts are whole words you know.","explain":"Snow and man are both real words, and a snowman is a man made of snow.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Is carpet a compound word?","choices":["No, a carpet is not a car or a pet","Yes, it has car and pet in it","Yes, because it is a long word"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the two little words help you know what a carpet is?","explain":"Car and pet hide in carpet, but they do not tell you what a carpet is. So carpet is not a compound."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some words are made of two words stuck together. Sun and shine make sunshine. Foot and ball make football. We call them compound words.","When you join the two words, keep every letter. Ear ends with r and ring starts with r. So earring has two r's. Write the first word, then the second word, with no gap.","To read a long word, look for the two words inside it. Toothbrush is tooth and brush. Cut it where both parts are real words. Too and thbrush does not work.","The two words help you know what the new word means. The last word tells you what the thing is. A houseboat is a boat you live in. A boathouse is a house for boats. Carpet has car and pet in it, but it is not a car or a pet, so it is not a compound."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 8. This matches the National Curriculum in England, English Appendix 1, Year 1 spelling: compound words are two words joined together, and each part of the longer word is spelt as it would be on its own (football, playground, farmyard, bedroom, blackberry). It also matches CCSS L.2.4d: use the meaning of each word to predict the meaning of a compound (birdhouse, lighthouse, housefly). Key words: compound word, root word, morpheme, closed compound, head word, syllable.","Most compounds children meet early are closed compounds, written as one word. In English the second part is usually the head: it names the kind of thing, and the first part narrows it down. So a goldfish is a fish and a houseboat is a boat, and swapping the parts changes the meaning. Some compounds are written with a space (ice cream) or a hyphen, and a few do not name their last part (a pickpocket is a person), but the joined, head-final kind is the one to teach first.","Common mistakes: dropping a letter where the words meet (earing for earring); splitting a compound in the wrong place when reading (too-thbrush); calling any long word with a little word inside a compound (carpet, button, season, target); writing the parts in the wrong order or with a gap; and making up a compound that is not a real word (rainsit). The lab, the practice and the challenge target each of these.","In the lab your child glues sun, foot and ear onto shine, ball and ring, and sees that earring keeps both r's. They cut cupcake, snowman, toothbrush and bedroom at the join, put house and boat in order for two different clues, sort playground, farmyard and blackberry from carpet, button and season, and find which endings make real words with rain. Words are said aloud when tapped, if the device can speak."]},"familyTip":"Go on a compound word hunt at home: toothbrush, bedroom, cupboard, doorbell, teapot. For each one, say the two words inside it and what they tell you. Then make up silly ones together, like a spoonbath or a sockhat, and ask whether they are real words or made-up ones.","keyIdea":"A compound word is two whole words joined to make one new word: sun + shine = sunshine. Each part keeps all its letters, so ear + ring = earring, with two r's. To read or spell a compound, find the two words inside it. The meanings of the two words help with the new meaning, and the last word usually says what the thing is: a goldfish is a fish. A word that only hides little words, like carpet, is not a compound.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a compound word? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Compound word","Not a compound"],"items":[["sunflower",0],["target",1],["lunchbox",0],["bandage",1],["bedtime",0],["rabbit",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcmpd","caption":"Flagship lab: glue two words into one, cut compounds at the join, order the parts, sort real compounds from tricks, find real rain words.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_C7abt7pRr6":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What sound does the e at the end of cake make?","choices":["No sound, it is silent","ee, like in see","e, like in egg"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say cake. Do you hear an e at the end?","explain":"The final e is silent. Its job is to make the a say its name."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word says the long a, like in its name?","choices":["tape","tap","top"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the magic e on the end.","explain":"tape has a–e, so the a says its name. tap has no e, so its a is short."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In tune, which letters make one sound together?","choices":["u and e","t and n","n and e"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the vowel and the e at the end.","explain":"u and e are a split digraph with the n in between: t, u–e, n."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which line is spelt right?","choices":["I fly my kite.","I fly my kit.","I fly my kiet."],"answer":0,"hint":"To fly it, the i needs to say its name.","explain":"kite needs the i–e split digraph. A kit is a set of things, not something you fly."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia reads {n} words. Each one has a split digraph. How many vowels say their name?","answer":"n","hint":"Each split digraph makes one vowel say its name.","explain":"One long vowel in each word, so {n} vowels say their name.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word does NOT have a split digraph?","choices":["road","rode","rope"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a split digraph, one letter sits between the vowel and the e.","explain":"In road, o and a sit side by side, so it is not a split digraph. rode and rope have o–e."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Tom has {n} cards: cap, kit, hop and more. He puts an e on the end of each. How many new words can he read?","answer":"n","hint":"Each card plus an e makes one new word.","explain":"Each of the {n} cards becomes one new word, like cap to cape, so that is {n} new words.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How many sounds are in slide?","choices":["4","5","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly: s, l, then the long i, then d.","explain":"s, l, i–e, d is 4 sounds in 5 letters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which pair shows a magic e changing a word?","choices":["cub and cube","cub and cup","cube and tube"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the pair where only an e is added.","explain":"cub + e = cube: the e makes the u say its name."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Put an e on the end of hop. What word do you get?","choices":["hope","hoop","hep"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the same letters and add e at the very end.","explain":"hop + e = hope. The e makes the o say its name."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Sam writes {n} words that have a magic e, like cake. How many silent e letters did Sam write?","answer":"n","hint":"Each magic e word has one silent e at the end.","explain":"One silent e in each word, so {n} words have {n} silent e letters.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many sounds are in bike?","choices":["3","4","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly. Do i and e make one sound together?","explain":"b, i–e, k: the i–e is one sound, so bike has 3 sounds and 4 letters."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A word has {n} letters and one split digraph. Every other letter makes its own sound. How many sounds does it have?","answer":"n-1","hint":"The vowel and the end e make just one sound together.","explain":"The two letters of the split digraph count as one sound, so {n} letters make {=n-1} sounds.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which word has a split digraph?","choices":["home","hot","boat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a vowel, one letter, then an e at the end.","explain":"In home, o and e make one sound with the m in between: h, o–e, m."}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at the word cap. Now put an e on the end. It says cape! The e makes no sound. But it makes the a say its name.","The a and the e are a team. They make one sound, even with the p between them. We call this a split digraph. Split means the two letters are apart.","There are five of them: a–e in cake, e–e in these, i–e in bike, o–e in home, and u–e in cube.","When you count sounds, the team counts as one. Cake has four letters. But it has three sounds: c, a–e, k. With no e, the vowel is short, like in tap. With the e, it is long, like in tape."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. The National Curriculum in England (English Appendix 1, Year 1) lists the split digraphs a–e, e–e, i–e, o–e and u–e among the graphemes children should read and spell, with examples such as made, these, five, home and rule. CCSS RF.1.3c asks first graders to know the final -e convention for long vowel sounds. Key terms: split digraph, grapheme, phoneme, short vowel, long vowel, consonant.","The rule taught here: when a single vowel is followed by one consonant and a final e, the e is silent and the vowel takes its long sound, often described as saying its name. The two letters are one grapheme for one phoneme, even though a consonant sits between them, so cake is 4 letters but 3 phonemes. u–e has two long sounds: /yoo/ as in cube and /oo/ as in rule or June; some speakers also say tune with /yoo/.","Common mistakes: sounding the final e out loud (kit-ee); counting each letter as a sound (cake as 4 sounds); reading a split digraph word with a short vowel, so hope is read as hop; leaving the e off in spelling, which can make a different real word (kit for kite); and confusing a split digraph with vowels side by side, such as oa in road. Be careful with common exception words like have, give, come and love, where the final e does not make the vowel long; English words rarely end in v, so the e is there for spelling.","In the lab your child drags an e onto cap, kit, hop and cub and sees the new word, taps the vowel and the e that make one sound, counts sounds in cake, bike, slide and home, finds the word in a sentence that should have an e, and sorts short and long vowel words. Each wrong tap gets a short reason. Phonics programmes may call it magic e, split digraph or vowel-consonant-e; it is the same idea."]},"familyTip":"Write cap, kit, hop and cub on scraps of paper and make one small card with an e on it. Take turns sliding the e onto the end and reading the new word out loud: cape, kite, hope, cube. Then hunt for split digraph words on food boxes and signs, like cake, bike, home or June, and draw a little bridge from the vowel over to the e.","keyIdea":"A split digraph is two letters that make one vowel sound with another letter in between them: the vowel and an e at the end of the word. Put an e on the end of cap and you get cape: the e is silent, but it makes the a say its name. The five split digraphs are a–e (cake), e–e (these), i–e (bike), o–e (home) and u–e (cube, tune). When you count the sounds in a word like cake, the a–e counts once: c, a–e, k is 3 sounds in 4 letters.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Short vowel or long vowel? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Short sound","Long: says its name"],"items":[["mad",0],["made",1],["hid",0],["hide",1],["rob",0],["robe",1],["tub",0],["tube",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmagice","caption":"Flagship lab: drag the magic e onto a word, join the split digraph, count sounds not letters, fix the word that lost its e, sort short from long vowels.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_roAgL1rQRF":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word is spelt right?","choices":["bright","brite","brigt"],"answer":0,"hint":"The middle sound is igh, three letters.","explain":"bright is b, r, igh, t. igh needs all three letters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is a trigraph?","choices":["3 letters that make 1 sound","3 sounds in a row","a word with 3 letters"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of igh in night: how many letters, and how many sounds?","explain":"A trigraph is three letters that make one sound, like igh, air and ear."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How many sounds are in high?","choices":["2","4","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it: h, then the igh sound.","explain":"high is h, igh: two sounds from four letters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which word has ear like in hear?","choices":["year","bear","wear"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one rhymes with hear?","explain":"year rhymes with hear. In bear and wear, ear sounds like air."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Ben writes {n} words with air, like hair. He colours each air team. How many teams does he colour?","answer":"n","hint":"Each word has one air team.","explain":"One air team in each word, so {n} words give {n} teams to colour.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word uses are for the air sound?","choices":["share","hair","fair"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the letters a, r, e.","explain":"share uses are. hair and fair make the same sound but use air."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"There are {n} cards with igh words. Zoe reads {k} of them. How many are left to read?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the cards she read away from all the cards.","explain":"{n} cards take away {k} read leaves {=n-k} cards.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which sentence is spelt right?","choices":["I can hear a bird.","I can heer a bird.","I can her a bird."],"answer":0,"hint":"The sound is ear. Which three letters write it?","explain":"hear uses the trigraph ear: h, ear."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"How many sounds are in night?","choices":["3","5","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hold up a finger for each sound. igh is one finger.","explain":"night is n, igh, t: three sounds from five letters."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which 3 letters make 1 sound in light?","choices":["igh","lig","ght"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say light slowly: l, then the middle sound, then t.","explain":"igh is the trigraph. light is l, igh, t: three sounds."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia reads {n} words. Each word has one trigraph, like igh in night. How many trigraphs does she read?","answer":"n","hint":"Each word has one 3-letter team.","explain":"One trigraph in each word, so {n} words have {n} trigraphs.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many sounds are in chair?","choices":["2","5","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"ch is one sound. air is one sound.","explain":"chair is ch, air: two sounds, even though it has five letters."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. Three of them are a trigraph. Every other letter makes its own sound. How many sounds does the word have?","answer":"n-2","hint":"The 3 letters of the trigraph count as just 1 sound.","explain":"Three letters become one sound, so {n} letters make {=n-2} sounds.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"In which word does ear sound like air?","choices":["pear","near","dear"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word rhymes with chair?","explain":"pear rhymes with chair, so its ear says air. near and dear say ear."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some sounds need three letters. Look at night. The letters i, g and h work as a team. Together they say igh. Three letters that make one sound are called a trigraph.","Here are four trigraphs. igh is in night. air is in chair. ear is in hear. are is in square.","To count the sounds in a word, say it slowly. Hold up one finger for each sound. Night is n, igh, t. That is three sounds, but five letters. The team gets one finger.","Watch out! ear can say two sounds. In hear and near it says ear. In bear and pear it says air. And air and are can sound the same. Chair uses air. Care uses are."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 6. The National Curriculum in England (English Appendix 1, Year 1) lists the trigraphs igh (high, night, light, bright, right), air (fair, pair, hair, chair), ear (dear, hear, near, year), ear with the air sound (bear, pear, wear) and are (bare, care, share, scared) among the graphemes children should read and spell. Letters and Sounds teaches igh, ear, air and ure as Phase 3 trigraphs. Key terms: phoneme (a sound), grapheme (the letters that write it), digraph (two letters, one sound), trigraph (three letters, one sound), segmenting and blending.","The idea that matters most is one-to-one: one phoneme, one grapheme. In night the grapheme igh writes one phoneme, so a child segments n, igh, t and blends those three back into the word. When counting, a trigraph gets one tap or one finger, just like a single letter or a digraph such as ch.","Common mistakes: sounding every letter, so night becomes n-i-g-h-t; counting letters instead of sounds; splitting the trigraph wrongly, as in reading the ch and then hai in chair; spelling by sound alone, as in nite, brite, heer and chare; and thinking ear always says the same sound, so bear is read to rhyme with hear. Be careful too with air and are, which make the same sound; there is no safe rule at this age, so learn the common words in sets.","In the lab your child drags igh, air, ear or are into a picture word (night, chair, light, hear, square), counts the sounds in five words by tapping a number, taps all three letters of the trigraph in five words, sorts six ear words into 'like hear' or 'like bear', and mends four sentences with a spelling slip. Every wrong move names the slip, for example '5 is how many letters'."]},"familyTip":"Hunt for trigraphs at bedtime. Say night, light, right and bright, and ask your child to hold up one finger for each sound: n, igh, t is three fingers, not five. Write the words on paper and colour igh in one colour so the three letters stay together. Then play 'hear or bear?': say near, pear, year and wear and sort them into two piles by the sound ear makes.","keyIdea":"A trigraph is three letters that make one sound. igh says the sound in night, air in chair, ear in hear, and are in square. When you count the sounds in a word, the three letters of a trigraph count as one: night has five letters but three sounds, n, igh, t. ear can make two sounds: ear as in hear and near, or air as in bear and pear. air and are can make the same sound, so learn which one each word uses: chair, but care.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which 3-letter team is in the word? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["igh, as in night","air or are, as in chair","ear, as in hear"],"items":[["light",0],["hair",1],["near",2],["high",0],["care",1],["year",2],["bright",0],["fair",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtgraph","caption":"Flagship lab: drag a 3-letter team into a picture word, count sounds not letters, find the trigraph, sort the two sounds of ear, and mend split spellings.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_14OR-MhGJ9":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a good first step with a long word you do not know?","choices":["Look for a part you already know","Guess from the first letter","Skip it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of know inside knowledge.","explain":"A known root, prefix or suffix makes a long word easier to read. Guessing from the first letter often gives the wrong word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How is women said?","choices":["wim-in","woe-men","wom-en"],"answer":0,"hint":"In this exception word the o makes an i sound.","explain":"women is said wim-in. The o makes an odd sound, so it is an exception word to learn."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"chef came from French. How does its ch sound?","choices":["sh","k","ch, like chip"],"answer":0,"hint":"French words with ch include machine and brochure.","explain":"In words from French, ch says sh: chef, machine, brochure. Greek words like chemist use k."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Where does disappear split into parts?","choices":["dis | appear","disa | ppear","di | sappear"],"answer":0,"hint":"dis is a prefix that means not.","explain":"dis + appear means not seen any more. The other splits leave no part you know."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A word family has {n} words, like sign, signal and signature. You can hear the g in {k} of them. In how many is the g silent?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the words where you hear the g away from the whole family.","explain":"{n} words take away {k} with a heard g leaves {=n-k} with a silent g.","params":{"n":[4,8],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why is there a w in two?","choices":["It belongs to the family of twin and twice","It is a spelling mistake","It makes the o long"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say twin. Can you hear the w?","explain":"two, twin and twice come from the same old word. The w is still heard in twin and twice."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which word you know helps you read heard?","choices":["hear","head","herd"],"answer":0,"hint":"heard is the past of a word you use with your ears.","explain":"heard is the past of hear, so it keeps the spelling of hear. herd sounds the same but is a group of animals."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How is answer said?","choices":["an-ser","an-swer","ans-wer"],"answer":0,"hint":"One letter in answer is silent.","explain":"The w in answer is silent, so it is said an-ser."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Lily reads {n} pages. Each page has 3 new long words. How many new long words does she read?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Three words on every page, so times the pages by 3.","explain":"{n} pages with 3 words each make {=3*n} words.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Which word helps explain the c in muscle?","choices":["muscular","mussel","music"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find a family word where the c is said as k.","explain":"You hear the c as k in muscular, which is from the muscle family. mussel is a sea creature, and music is not related."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word you know is inside weight?","choices":["weigh","eight","wig"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what weight means: how much a thing weighs.","explain":"weight is how much something weighs, so weigh is the root. eight is inside too, but it does not give the meaning."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A list has {n} exception words. You can read {k} of them already. How many are left to learn?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the words you can read away from the whole list.","explain":"{n} words take away {k} you know leaves {=n-k} to learn.","params":{"n":[10,20],"k":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"chorus came from Greek. How does its ch sound?","choices":["k","sh","ch, like chip"],"answer":0,"hint":"Greek words with ch are like chemist and echo.","explain":"In words from Greek, ch says k: chorus, chemist, echo. French words such as chef use sh."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ravi splits {n} long words into two parts each, like dis + appear. How many parts does he read?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each word makes two parts.","explain":"{n} words with 2 parts each make {=2*n} parts.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which word explains the silent g in sign?","choices":["signal","sing","sight"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find a word from the same family where you can hear the g.","explain":"You hear the g in signal, and sign and signal are one family. sing is not related, and sight has a silent gh."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some long words look scary. But most of them have a part you already know. Look at knowledge. It has know inside it. Knowledge is what you know, so the k is silent, just like in know.","Many words have a start or an end you can split off. Disappear is dis and appear. Actually is actual and ly. Read each part, then put them back together.","A silent letter is often there for a reason. You can't hear the w in two. But you can hear it in twin and twice, which come from the same family. You hear the g in signal, so sign has a g too.","Where a word came from can change a sound. In words from Greek, ch says k, as in echo. In words from French, it says sh, as in chef. Some words just have one odd part, like the s in island. Learn that part, and read the rest as normal."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. In Years 3 and 4, the National Curriculum in England asks children to use root words, prefixes and suffixes to read new words. This is called morphology. They also use where words came from, which is called etymology. They read more exception words too, and notice the parts that break the usual rules. The Year 3 and 4 list includes island, knowledge, guard, heart, busy, women, eighth and weight. It also shows ch from Greek (chemist, echo, chorus) and from French (chef, machine, brochure).","By now most children can blend short words. Long words need more tools. Spot a root you know, such as know in knowledge or hear in heard. Peel off a prefix or a suffix, such as dis-, bi- or -ly. Use a word from the same family to explain a silent letter: two and twin, sign and signal, muscle and muscular. Use the origin to pick a sound for ch. Then check that the word makes sense in the sentence.","Common mistakes: saying every letter of an exception word, such as the s in island or the w in answer. Guessing from the first letter only. Splitting a word where no part means anything (bic-ycle). Using the usual ch sound in chef or chemist. Be careful not to teach every odd word as a whole shape to memorise. Showing the root or the family word explains the spelling, and that helps with new words too.","In the lab your child drags a known word onto six long words. They find a family word where four silent letters can be heard. They sort six ch words by Greek or French origin, and match six exception words to how they sound. Last, they tap the gap that splits four words into parts that mean something."]},"familyTip":"When your child gets stuck on a long word, ask 'Is there a word you know inside it?' before telling them. Cover the prefix or suffix with a finger, read the middle, then add the parts back. Collect 'word families' on a sticky note: sign, signal, signature; two, twin, twice.","keyIdea":"To read a long or odd-looking word, look for parts you already know. A root word inside helps: know is in knowledge, so the k is silent. Prefixes and suffixes split off: dis + appear, actual + ly. A silent letter often makes sense in a related word: the w in two is heard in twin, and the g in sign is heard in signal. Where a word came from matters too: ch says k in Greek words like chemist and echo, but sh in French words like chef and machine. Exception words such as island, women and busy have one odd part to learn; read the rest as usual.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What is odd about each exception word? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["has a silent letter","a vowel makes an odd sound"],"items":[["island",0],["women",1],["answer",0],["busy",1],["guard",0],["heart",1],["knowledge",0],["breath",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdecode","caption":"Flagship lab: find a known word inside, hear silent letters in relatives, sort ch by origin, match exception words, split off parts.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_a6m7PqTuJN":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Put r after the a in cat. What word do you get?","choices":["cart","crat","catr"],"answer":0,"hint":"The r goes right after the vowel a.","explain":"c, a, r, t: cart. The a and the r make one sound, ar."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word has the same sound as fork?","choices":["corn","card","curl"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for o and r together.","explain":"corn has or, like fork. card has ar and curl has ur."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which letters make one sound in shirt?","choices":["ir","sh and ir","rt"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the vowel with r right after it.","explain":"i and r go together: sh, ir, t. sh is a different sound."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A word has {n} letters and ends in ore, like more. Every other letter makes its own sound. How many sounds does it have?","answer":"n-2","hint":"o, r and e make one sound together, so three letters count as one.","explain":"{n} letters, but ore counts once, so {=n-2} sounds. more has 4 letters and 2 sounds: m, ore.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia has {a} cards with ir words, {b} with ur words and {c} with er words. How many cards say the same sound as her?","answer":"a+b+c","hint":"ir, ur and er all say the same sound.","explain":"All three spellings say the sound in her, so {a} + {b} + {c} = {=a+b+c} cards.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4],"c":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"I fell over. It will ...","choices":["hurt","hut","hurd"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means it gives you pain?","explain":"hurt has ur. hut is a little house, and hurd is not a word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"She looks after sick people. Which spelling is right?","choices":["nurse","nirse","nruse"],"answer":0,"hint":"ir and ur sound the same. nurse uses one of them, after the vowel.","explain":"nurse is spelt n, ur, se. nirse has the wrong letters, and nruse has the r too soon."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How many sounds are in star?","choices":["3","4","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"s, t, then a and r together.","explain":"star has 4 letters but 3 sounds: s, t, ar."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"The pet that says meow is a ...","choices":["cat","cart","curt"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does this word need an r?","explain":"A cat says meow. cart has an r and means something with wheels."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Which word ends with the same sound as more?","choices":["shore","share","sure"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for o, r, e at the end.","explain":"shore ends in ore, like more. share ends in are, and sure sounds different."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word has the same sound as car?","choices":["farm","fork","fan"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the word with a and r together.","explain":"farm has ar, the sound in car. fork has or, and fan has no r."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. Two of them are a and r together, and every other letter makes its own sound. How many sounds does the word have?","answer":"n-1","hint":"a and r make one sound, so two letters count as one.","explain":"{n} letters, but ar counts once, so {=n-1} sounds. car has 3 letters and 2 sounds: c, ar.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"bird, burn and her. What is the same?","choices":["the middle sound","the spelling of the sound","the first letter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say them out loud. Then look at the letters.","explain":"ir, ur and er make the same sound, but they are spelt with different letters."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A word has {n} letters, like bun. You put r right after the vowel, like burn. How many letters does the new word have?","answer":"n+1","hint":"You add one letter. Count it.","explain":"{n} letters plus the r gives {=n+1} letters. The number of sounds stays {n}, because u and r say one sound.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is spelt right?","choices":["girl","gurl","gril"],"answer":0,"hint":"ir and ur sound the same. Which one does girl use? The r comes after the vowel.","explain":"girl is spelt g, ir, l. gurl has the wrong letters for the sound, and gril has the r before the i."}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at cat. Now put an r after the a. You get cart. The a and the r say a new sound together: ar.","ar says the sound in car and star. or says the sound in fork and corn. ore says it too, like in more.","ir, ur and er all say the same sound. Listen: bird, burn, her. They sound the same, but they look different. We learn which one each word has.","Two letters, one sound. In bird, i and r go together. So bird has four letters but three sounds: b, ir, d."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 6. The National Curriculum in England teaches these graphemes in Year 1 (English Appendix 1): ar (car, start, park, arm, garden), or (for, short, born, horse, morning), ore (more, score, before, wore, shore), er (her, term, verb, person), ir (girl, bird, shirt, first, third) and ur (turn, hurt, church, burst, Thursday). In US phonics these are r-controlled vowels (Common Core RF.1.3 and RF.2.3), and a syllable with one is an r-controlled syllable.","The r changes the vowel, so the vowel and r are read as one phoneme. In standard British English the r is not sounded separately at all; in American English it is blended into the vowel. Either way, cart has three phonemes, c, ar, t, not four. ir, ur and er spell the same sound, so the spelling of each word has to be learned and checked; in most words er comes at the end (her, letter) while ir and ur come in the middle.","Common mistakes: reading the short vowel and then a separate r (c-a-r-t as four sounds, or reading cart as cat), reversing the letters (crat, gril, hrut), choosing the wrong one of ir, ur and er (gurl, nirse, burd), writing or for ore at the end (mor), and counting letters instead of sounds when segmenting.","In the lab your child slides an r tile into four short words to make new ones (cat to cart, bun to burn, bid to bird, shot to short), sorts nine words into ar, or and er sound boxes, taps the letters that make the sound in six words, picks ir or ur, or or ar for four gaps, and decides in five sentences whether the word needs an r."]},"familyTip":"Go on an r hunt in a picture book. Your child finds words like car, star, corn, bird or her, and puts a finger under the vowel and the r together while saying the one sound. Then play 'add an r': say cat, and ask what you get with r after the a (cart). Try bun to burn and shot to short.","keyIdea":"When r comes right after a vowel, the vowel and the r make one new sound together. ar says the sound in car and farm. or says the sound in fork and corn, and ore at the end of a word says the same sound, as in more. ir, ur and er all say the same sound, as in bird, burn and her, so you have to learn which spelling each word uses. Adding r changes the word: cat becomes cart, bun becomes burn. Two letters, one sound: bird has four letters but three sounds, b, ir, d.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which sound does each word have? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["ar as in car","or as in fork","er as in her"],"items":[["star",0],["corn",1],["bird",2],["farm",0],["more",1],["turn",2],["horse",1],["fern",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrvowel","caption":"Flagship lab: slide an r in after the vowel to make new words, sort words by sound, tap the letters that make the sound, and pick the right spelling.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_fMd7v87IiI":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word has ow like in cow?","choices":["town","grow","show"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say cow. Listen for that sound.","explain":"town has ow like cow. grow and show have ow like snow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which two words have the same vowel sound?","choices":["cloud and cow","cloud and blue","cow and snow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Different letters can make the same sound. Say them out loud.","explain":"ou in cloud and ow in cow say the same sound. cow and snow both have ow, but it says two different sounds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which letters make one sound in claw?","choices":["aw","cl","law"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the two letters that make the vowel sound.","explain":"a and w say one sound: cl, aw. c and l each say their own sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In which word does y say ee?","choices":["funny","fly","yes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clap the words. Two beats at the end means ee.","explain":"funny has two beats, so y says ee. fly has one beat, so y says igh. In yes, y is at the start and is not a vowel."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"It is white and floats in the sky. Which spelling is right?","choices":["cloud","clowd","clawd"],"answer":0,"hint":"ou and ow sound the same here. This word uses ou.","explain":"cloud is spelt with ou. clowd makes the same sound but is not how the word is spelt, and aw is a different sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You can eat it. It has a crust. Which word is it?","choices":["pie","paw","pew"],"answer":0,"hint":"ie says igh, like in tie.","explain":"pie has ie saying igh. A paw is a dog's foot, and a pew is a church seat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mix the ___ to make bread. Which word fits?","choices":["flour","flower","floor"],"answer":0,"hint":"flour and flower sound the same. Which one is for baking?","explain":"flour is the white powder for bread. A flower grows in the garden, and the floor is what you stand on."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. It has ew saying one sound, and every other letter says its own sound. How many sounds does it have?","answer":"n-1","hint":"e and w together make one sound.","explain":"{n} letters, but ew counts once, so {=n-1} sounds. flew has 4 letters and 3 sounds: f, l, ew.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"You read {n} words that end in y. Each one has two beats, like happy and funny. In how many does y say ee?","answer":"n","hint":"Two beats at the end of the word means y says ee.","explain":"All {n} have two beats, so y says ee in all {n}, like happy, funny and sunny.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Read the word: blow. What does ow say?","choices":["the sound in snow","the sound in cow","the sound in saw"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try both ow sounds. Which one makes a real word?","explain":"blow has ow like snow. Said like cow, it is not the word you know."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word has ow like in snow?","choices":["slow","now","brown"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word. Listen for the sound at the end of snow.","explain":"slow has ow like snow. now and brown have ow like cow."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. Two of them are o and u together, saying one sound. Every other letter makes its own sound. How many sounds does the word have?","answer":"n-1","hint":"o and u say one sound, so two letters count as one.","explain":"{n} letters, but ou counts once, so {=n-1} sounds. cloud has 5 letters and 4 sounds: c, l, ou, d.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which word has the same sound as blue?","choices":["flew","blow","claw"],"answer":0,"hint":"ue and ew can say the same sound.","explain":"flew has ew, the same sound as ue in blue. blow has ow like snow, and claw has aw."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You find {a} words that end in y and have one beat, like my, and {b} that end in y and have two beats, like happy. In how many does y say igh?","answer":"a","hint":"One beat, like my, means y says igh. Two beats, like happy, means y says ee.","explain":"Only the {a} one-beat words say igh. The {b} two-beat words say ee at the end.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"The sky is ___. Which word fits?","choices":["blue","blew","blow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it a colour, or something the wind did?","explain":"blue is the colour. blew is what the wind did. They sound the same but mean different things."}],"explain":{"kid":["Say cow. Now say snow. Both have ow, but ow says a different sound in each. When you see ow, try both sounds. Pick the one that makes a real word.","Some sounds have two spellings. cloud has ou and cow has ow, but they say the same sound. flew has ew and blue has ue. They say the same sound too. So do aw in saw and au in sauce.","Look at y at the end of a word. Clap it. my has one beat, so y says igh. happy has two beats, so y says ee.","blue and blew sound the same. blue is a colour. The wind blew. The meaning tells you which one to write."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 6. The National Curriculum in England (English Appendix 1, Year 1) teaches ou (out, about, cloud), ow for two sounds (now, brown, town and own, blow, snow), ue and ew (blue, clue, true; new, flew, grew), ie as igh (lie, tie, pie), aw and au (saw, draw, yawn; author, August) and y at the end saying ee (very, happy, funny). y saying igh at the end of words (cry, fly, dry) is in Year 2. In the US these are vowel teams and diphthongs (Common Core RF.1.3c and RF.2.3b).","Strictly, a diphthong is a vowel that glides from one sound to another inside one syllable, like ow in cow, ie in pie and y in my. ew, ue, aw and au are long vowels rather than glides, which is why the curriculum groups them all as vowel digraphs. The sound of aw and au varies by accent: in much of North America saw and sauce rhyme with the vowel in father or law, but the two spellings still share one sound.","Common mistakes: reading every ow as in cow (so snow rhymes with cow), thinking ou and ow must make different sounds because they look different, choosing the wrong spelling for a shared sound (clowd, blew for the colour), reading y at the start of a word as a vowel, and saying igh for the y in happy. The beat rule for y works for most early words; a few two-beat words such as July and reply end in igh and are learnt by sight.","In the lab your child sorts eight ow words into cow and snow houses, sorts nine words by shared sound, drags ow, ou, ew, aw or ie into a word gap from a clue, sorts eight y words by the number of beats, and picks between sound-alike words such as blew and blue, flower and flour, tied and tide."]},"familyTip":"Write cow and snow on two cards. Read a list together (town, grow, down, show) and ask your child which card each word goes with. If they are stuck, say the word both ways and ask which one is a real word. Then hunt for y at the end of words in a book and clap the beats: one beat says igh (my), two beats say ee (happy).","keyIdea":"Some vowel sounds are spelt with two letters, and some letter pairs can say more than one sound. ow says the sound in cow and also the sound in snow, so a reader tries both and picks the real word. Some sounds have two spellings: ou and ow (cloud, cow), ew and ue (flew, blue), aw and au (saw, sauce). ie says igh in pie. At the end of a word, y says igh in a short word like my and ee in a longer word like happy. Words that sound the same can be spelt differently, so the meaning tells you which one to write: blue or blew.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which sound does each word have? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["ow as in cow","ow as in snow"],"items":[["town",0],["grow",1],["owl",0],["slow",1],["down",0],["show",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagglide","caption":"Flagship lab: sort ow words, match two spellings of one sound, fill a word gap, sort y endings by beats, pick between sound-alikes.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt__CMXZiPfTV":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word has a real ending added?","choices":["taller","sister","letter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cover er. Is a word you know left?","explain":"Cover er in taller and tall is left. sist and lett are not words, so sister and letter just end in er."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"running comes from which root?","choices":["run","rune","runn"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two n's before ing mean a short vowel.","explain":"The n was doubled before ing, so the root is run, with a short u."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How many beats does jumped have?","choices":["1","2","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"jump does not end in t or d.","explain":"jumped sounds like jumpt. ed adds a t sound, not a beat, so it has one beat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How do you split coldest to read it?","choices":["cold + est","col + dest","co + ldest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the root first, then the ending after it.","explain":"Cover est and cold is left. Read the root, then the ending: cold + est."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word has an ending that adds a beat?","choices":["wishes","cats","helps"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clap each word. Listen for an extra beat at the end.","explain":"wish ends in sh, so es adds a beat: wish-es. cats and helps have one beat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Read the word unlocked. Which chunks are in it?","choices":["un + lock + ed","unl + ock + ed","un + locked + ed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the root in the middle, then the parts on each side.","explain":"lock is the root, un is at the front and ed is at the end: un + lock + ed."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A root has {n} letters and ends in a short vowel and one letter, like hop. You double the last letter and add ing. How many letters now?","answer":"n+4","hint":"One extra letter for the double, three for ing.","explain":"{n} letters, plus 1 for the doubled letter, plus 3 for ing, gives {=n+4}. hop has 3 and hopping has 7.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A sentence has {a} words. {b} of them have an ending added. How many words have no ending?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the words with endings away from all the words.","explain":"{a} words take away the {b} with endings leaves {=a-b} without an ending.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which word is a root on its own, with no ending?","choices":["king","singing","rings"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cover ing in each one. Is a word you know left?","explain":"Cover ing in king and only k is left, so king has no ending. singing is sing + ing, and rings is ring + s."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is the root in jumping?","choices":["jump","jum","ping"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cover ing. What word is left?","explain":"Cover ing and jump is left, a word you know. jum-ping splits by sound, not by root."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A root has {n} letters and does not change when you add ing, like jump and jumping. How many letters does the new word have?","answer":"n+3","hint":"ing has three letters, and the root keeps all of its letters.","explain":"{n} letters in the root plus 3 for ing gives {=n+3}. jump has 4 and jumping has 7.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many beats does landed have?","choices":["2","1","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does land end in t or d? Then ed adds a beat.","explain":"land ends in d, so ed adds a beat: land-ed has two beats."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You read {a} words ending in ed. {b} of them have a root ending in t or d, like wanted. How many of the words have just one beat, like jumped?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Only roots ending in t or d get an extra beat from ed.","explain":"{b} words get the extra beat, so {a} − {b} = {=a-b} words have one beat, like jumped.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What is the root in hoping?","choices":["hope","hop","hoppe"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there one p or two before the ending?","explain":"hoping has one p, so the root lost its e: hope. hop would make hopping, with two p's."}],"explain":{"kid":["A long word is often a root with an ending on the back. Cover the ending with your finger. Read the root. Then add the ending: jump, jumping.","Some endings add a beat. Clap box, then boxes: box-es. ed adds a beat only when the root ends in t or d: want-ed, land-ed. jumped has one beat. It sounds like jumpt.","Look for a double letter. hopping has two p's, so it comes from hop. hoping has one p, so it comes from hope. The e dropped off.","Not every word that ends in ing or er has an ending. Cover ing in king: k is not a word. So king is just king."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. The National Curriculum in England (Year 1 word reading) asks children to read words containing taught GPCs and -s, -es, -ing, -ed, -er and -est endings, and other words of more than one syllable. Year 2 adds reading and spelling words where the root changes: doubling the consonant after a short vowel (hopping, patted) and dropping e (hoping, liked). In the US this is Common Core RF.1.3f and RF.1.3g (read two-syllable words, read inflectional endings) and RF.2.3d (common prefixes and suffixes).","The terms: the root (base word) carries the meaning and an inflectional ending (an inflection, or inflectional suffix) adds number, tense or comparison without changing the word class. The past tense -ed has three pronunciations: /ɪd/ after t or d (wanted, landed), /t/ after other voiceless sounds (jumped, looked) and /d/ after voiced sounds (played, hugged). Plural -es is /ɪz/ after s, x, z, sh and ch, so it adds a syllable; plain -s does not.","Common mistakes: splitting by syllable instead of by root (col-dest, jum-ping), saying jump-ed with an extra beat, reading every final ing or er as an ending (king, sister, letter), and reading hoping as hopping because the root e is gone. The fix is the same each time: cover the ending and check that a real root is left.","In the lab your child taps the first letter of the ending in six words, taps a drum once for each beat in seven words, picks the root of seven doubled or e-dropped words (hopping, hoping, tapping, taped), sorts eight words into a real ending or just ends that way, and taps every word with an ending in four sentences, including traps like sister and king."]},"familyTip":"Pick a word with an ending from a book page. Put your finger over the ending so only the root shows, read the root together, then lift your finger and read the whole word. Clap it: does the ending add a beat? Try hopping and hoping side by side and ask which one a rabbit does.","keyIdea":"To read a long word, find the root first and then add the ending: jump + ing, cold + est, box + es, and un + lock + ed for a word with a part at the front too. Split at the root, not where the sounds seem to break (cold + est, not col + dest). Some endings add a beat: -es (box-es), and -ed after a root that ends in t or d (want-ed, land-ed); after other sounds -ed adds only a t or d sound (jumpt, playd). A double letter before the ending means the root has a short vowel (hopping from hop), and one letter can mean the root lost an e (hoping from hope). Cover the ending to check: if no word you know is left, it is not an ending (king, sister).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the ending add a beat? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["one beat","two beats"],"items":[["jumped",0],["wanted",1],["played",0],["landed",1],["cats",0],["boxes",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagendread","caption":"Flagship lab: find where the ending starts, drum the beats, find the root of doubled words, sort look-alikes, and spot endings in sentences.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_kVzAFMuFc4":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where does rabbit split?","choices":["rab | bit","ra | bbit","rabb | it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two consonants in the middle: cut between them.","explain":"Rab-bit splits between the two b letters. Each part has one vowel sound, and both vowels are short."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How many syllables does teapot have?","choices":["2 syllables","3 syllables","1 syllable"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the vowel sounds. Is ea one sound?","explain":"Tea-pot has two vowel sounds, ea and o, so two syllables. The ea team counts once, so it is not three."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where does robot split?","choices":["ro | bot","rob | ot","r | obot"],"answer":0,"hint":"One consonant in the middle. Try open first.","explain":"Ro-bot: try open first, and ro with a long o makes a real word. Rob-ot does not, and r has no vowel."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Where does lemon split?","choices":["lem | on","le | mon","l | emon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try open first: lee-mon. Is that a real word? If not, flex.","explain":"Lee-mon is not a word, so move the cut: lem-on, with a short e. That is the word you know."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where does puddle split?","choices":["pud | dle","pudd | le","pu | ddle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Consonant-le stays together. Find the letter just before le.","explain":"Pud-dle: the d before le joins it to make dle, and pud keeps the other d. Pudd-le breaks up the consonant-le."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ava splits {n} words. She cuts pilot as p | ilot. What is wrong?","choices":["The part p has no vowel","Pilot has no vowels","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at each part. Does each one have a vowel sound?","explain":"Every syllable needs a vowel sound, and p has none. The right cut is pi-lot, an open first syllable with a long i.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ben has {n} words to sort. Is the first part of paper open or closed?","choices":["Open: pa ends in a vowel, so the a is long","Closed: pa ends in a consonant","Closed: paper has two p letters"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last letter of pa.","explain":"Pa ends in the vowel a, so it is open and the a says its name. Closed syllables end in a consonant, like nap in napkin.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Where does basket split?","choices":["bas | ket","ba | sket","bask | et"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the two consonants between the vowels a and e.","explain":"The consonants s and k sit between the vowels, so cut between them: bas-ket. Both parts are closed, with short vowels."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"How many syllables does sunset have?","choices":["2 syllables","6 syllables","1 syllable"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the vowel sounds, u and e.","explain":"Sun-set has two vowel sounds, so two syllables. Six is how many letters it has, which is a different count."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where does napkin split into syllables?","choices":["nap | kin","na | pkin","napk | in"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the two consonants in the middle, p and k. Cut between them.","explain":"Nap-kin: two consonants sit between the vowels, so the cut goes between them. Both parts are closed, so the a and i are short."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Leo has {n} word cards. One says rain. He says rain has 2 syllables, because it has a and i. Is he right?","choices":["No, ai is one vowel sound, so 1 syllable","Yes, 2 vowels make 2 syllables","No, rain has 4 syllables"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say rain. Is the ai one sound or two?","explain":"The a and i in rain are a vowel team. They make one sound, so rain has one syllable. Count sounds, not letters.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which way does tiger make a real word?","choices":["ti | ger, with a long i","tig | er, with a short i","t | iger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try the open cut first: the first part ends in a vowel, so the vowel says its name.","explain":"Ti-ger: ti is an open syllable, so the i says its name. Tig-er does not sound like a word, and t has no vowel."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia reads {n} words with le at the end. Where does candle split?","choices":["can | dle","cand | le","ca | ndle"],"answer":0,"hint":"The le takes the consonant just before it: dle.","explain":"Can-dle: consonant-le stays together as dle, and the n goes with can. Cand-le splits the consonant from its le.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Cabin has one consonant between the vowels. Ca-bin sounds like cay-bin. What should you try next?","choices":["Cut after the b: cab-in, with a short a","Cut after the c: c-abin","Give up on the word"],"answer":0,"hint":"If the open try is not a real word, move the cut one letter on.","explain":"Cay-bin is not a word, so flex: cab-in makes the first syllable closed, with a short a. Now it sounds right. C-abin leaves a part with no vowel."}],"explain":{"kid":["Long words are easier to read in parts. Each part is a syllable. Each syllable has one vowel sound. So find the vowels first: a, e, i, o and u.","Two vowels side by side often make one sound, like ai in rain or oa in boat. That counts as one vowel sound, so rain has one syllable.","Look between the vowels. Two consonants? Cut between them: nap-kin, rab-bit. One consonant? Try ti-ger first, where the i says its name. If it does not sound right, cut after it: cab-in.","A word that ends with a consonant and le keeps them together: ta-ble, can-dle. Now read each part and say them fast. You just read a long word!"],"adult":["Ages 6 to 9. This is the decoding step after children can hear and clap syllables. Common Core RF.1.3d and RF.1.3e ask children to use the fact that every syllable has a vowel sound and to decode two-syllable words by breaking them into syllables; RF.2.3c and RF.3.3c extend this to long vowels and longer words. In England, Year 1 children read words of more than one syllable containing taught grapheme-phoneme correspondences, and Years 2 to 4 build fluency with longer words.","The patterns are named by the letters between the vowels. VC/CV: two consonants, split between them (bas-ket), giving closed syllables with short vowels. V/CV: one consonant, first try an open syllable that ends in the vowel, which is long (mu-sic). VC/V: if the open try is not a real word, move the cut so the first syllable is closed and short (lem-on). Consonant-le: the consonant before le joins it (pud-dle). Vowel teams such as ai, ea and oa are never split.","These are reading strategies, not spelling laws or dictionary hyphenation. Their job is to give a child a pronounceable try that they then check against words they know. That is why the V/CV rule says try open first and flex if it does not make sense.","Common mistakes: counting vowel letters instead of vowel sounds (rain is one syllable), leaving a part with no vowel (p-ilot), always cutting after the first vowel (ca-bin read as cay-bin), and cutting consonant-le the wrong way (tab-le). In the lab your child marks vowel sounds, cuts words at the gaps, picks open or closed, keeps le with its consonant and sorts first syllables."]},"familyTip":"When your child gets stuck on a long word in a book, cover part of it with your finger. Ask them to find the vowels, read the first part, then slide your finger along and read the next. Then say it fast. Try it on a cereal box or a sign: pic-nic, can-dle, ro-bot.","keyIdea":"Every syllable has one vowel sound, so to read a long word, find the vowels and cut the word into parts. Two consonants in the middle: cut between them (nap-kin). One consonant in the middle: try an open cut first, where the first part ends in a vowel and the vowel says its name (ti-ger); if that is not a real word, try a closed cut with a short vowel (cab-in). A word ending in a consonant plus le keeps those three letters together (ta-ble). Read each part, then blend them into the word.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Count the vowel sounds. How many syllables? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["1 syllable","2 syllables","3 syllables"],"items":[["rain",0],["boat",0],["sunset",1],["robot",1],["teapot",1],["magnet",1],["fantastic",2],["animal",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsyldiv","caption":"Flagship lab: tap the vowel sounds, cut between two consonants, try open then closed, keep consonant-le together, and sort open and closed syllables.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
